WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.368 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:03.368 --> 00:00:04.324 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.324 --> 00:00:05.280 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.347 align:middle line:90%


00:00:09.347 --> 00:00:10.180 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK, great.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.440 align:middle line:84%
So let's now say a little bit
about what this course is.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.400 align:middle line:84%
This is a course on game theory
or on the economic applications

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.440 align:middle line:90%
of game theory.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.080 align:middle line:84%
So I think a natural
starting point

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.760 align:middle line:90%
is to ask what is game theory.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.840 align:middle line:84%
Well, here's two
definitions, both from people

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.320 align:middle line:84%
who've won the Nobel Prize
for their work in game theory.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.880 align:middle line:84%
The first is by
Roger Myerson, who

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.800 align:middle line:84%
says that game theory is the
study of mathematical models

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.160 align:middle line:84%
of conflict and cooperation
between intelligent, rational

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.120 align:middle line:90%
decision makers.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.660 align:middle line:84%
Robert Aumann, also
a Nobel laureate,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.400 align:middle line:84%
gives just a briefer
definition, which I think

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.560 align:middle line:90%
encapsulates the key ideas.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.440 align:middle line:84%
It's interactive
decision theory.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.970 align:middle line:84%
It's about people
making decisions

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.220 align:middle line:90%
when their decisions interact.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.500 align:middle line:84%
What does it mean for people's
decisions to interact?

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.180 align:middle line:84%
Well, I think the best way
to see it is just an example.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.847 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
play a game as a class

00:00:58.847 --> 00:01:00.440 align:middle line:84%
to see what it means
to be a hopefully

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.290 align:middle line:84%
rational and intelligent
interacting decision maker.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.540 align:middle line:84%
And then after that, I'll
get into the formal material

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:06.300 align:middle line:90%
on the board.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.800 align:middle line:84%
So to play games
in the class, we're

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.365 align:middle line:84%
going to use a
platform called Moblab.

00:01:10.365 --> 00:01:12.740 align:middle line:84%
So let me just describe the
game and then I'll hit Begin.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.900 align:middle line:84%
You're each going to be assigned
a random number between 1

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:15.460 align:middle line:90%
and 100.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.680 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be an integer.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.080 align:middle line:84%
And then you're each going to
make a guess, which is also

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.500 align:middle line:90%
a number between 1 and 100.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:21.760 align:middle line:90%
The winner of the game--

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.240 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, there's no
real prizes but pride--

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.800 align:middle line:84%
the winner of the game is the
person who guesses closest

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.085 align:middle line:84%
to 2/3 of the average of
everyone's number in the class.

00:01:30.085 --> 00:01:31.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The number
they guess or the number

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.267 align:middle line:90%
they were assigned?

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:34.600 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: The number they guess.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.740 align:middle line:90%
So you're assigned a number.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:36.240 align:middle line:90%
Only you know that.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.500 align:middle line:90%
No one else knows that number.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.708 align:middle line:90%
You're going to make a guess.

00:01:38.708 --> 00:01:41.800 align:middle line:84%
And the winning guess is the
guess that's closest to 2/3

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.060 align:middle line:84%
of the average of everyone
in the class's guess.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:44.760 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:45.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is
everyone's number drawn

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:46.887 align:middle line:90%
from a uniform distribution?

00:01:46.887 --> 00:01:47.947 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yes.

00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:48.780 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:49.080 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:49.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's
the range again?

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:50.920 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: 1 to 100.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:51.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.660 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.700 align:middle line:84%
I think these details
should be there,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.938 align:middle line:84%
but sometimes they're
a little imprecise

00:01:56.938 --> 00:01:57.980 align:middle line:90%
in the way they write it.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.540 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's begin.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.680 align:middle line:90%


00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:05.760 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.800 align:middle line:90%
Here we are.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.660 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.172 align:middle line:84%
So here on the
horizontal axis are

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:12.380 align:middle line:90%
the guesses that people made.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:13.797 align:middle line:84%
Again, to clarify,
not the numbers

00:02:13.797 --> 00:02:15.820 align:middle line:84%
you got, but the guesses
that people are making.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.487 align:middle line:84%
And on the vertical axis, we're
showing the percentage of people

00:02:18.487 --> 00:02:20.000 align:middle line:90%
who made that particular guess.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:20.987 align:middle line:90%
So we have a few.

00:02:20.987 --> 00:02:22.820 align:middle line:84%
It's a bit maybe hard
to see with the color,

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.600 align:middle line:84%
but we have a few
summary statistics here.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.160 align:middle line:84%
We see that the
average was 36 roughly.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.720 align:middle line:84%
And that's the dotted
purple line here.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.640 align:middle line:84%
And the winning guess was the
guess that was closest to 2/3

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.080 align:middle line:90%
of 36, so about 24.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.640 align:middle line:90%
And we see that guess is here.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.160 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone want to share
their thought process?

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.660 align:middle line:84%
Maybe someone who was
quite high up here,

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.560 align:middle line:90%
what they were thinking.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.220 align:middle line:90%
Or low?

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:54.543 align:middle line:84%
Anyone who's willing to share
what they were thinking about?

00:02:54.543 --> 00:02:55.460 align:middle line:90%
What about the winner?

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.960 align:middle line:84%
They should be able
to come forward.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:57.650 align:middle line:90%
Who won?

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.150 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:58.650 align:middle line:90%
You won.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.617 align:middle line:84%
OK, tell me, what
was your reasoning?

00:03:00.617 --> 00:03:02.450 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I wouldn't
replicate the strategy.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:03.890 align:middle line:90%
I think it's not the best idea.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.790 align:middle line:84%
But what I did was put the
mean of a uniform distribution

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.730 align:middle line:84%
from 0 to 100 would
probably be around 50.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:10.478 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:03:10.478 --> 00:03:11.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'd take 2/3 of that.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.478 align:middle line:84%
But I thought probably
other people would

00:03:13.478 --> 00:03:15.030 align:middle line:90%
be thinking the same thing.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.570 align:middle line:84%
So I downscaled it
a bit more by 2/3.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:18.590 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Great.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.090 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.330 align:middle line:84%
So you're showing the foundation
of game theoretic reasoning,

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.010 align:middle line:84%
what we talked about,
interactive decision making.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.590 align:middle line:84%
You didn't just think
about your number.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.330 align:middle line:84%
You had to think about
other people's numbers.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And then you had to think about
what other people will do given

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.450 align:middle line:90%
the numbers that they got.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.290 align:middle line:84%
And you adjusted your
strategy accordingly.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.670 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, it
worked out pretty well.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:37.430 align:middle line:84%
Anyone else want to share
maybe a bid they're happy with,

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.630 align:middle line:84%
a bid they feel they
made a mistake on?

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.430 align:middle line:90%
Any other thoughts?

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.150 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.772 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I did 26.

00:03:44.772 --> 00:03:45.480 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: 26, OK.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.510 align:middle line:90%
So you were pretty close, yeah?

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:48.467 align:middle line:84%
I thought maybe
something like 30,

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:50.150 align:middle line:84%
and then figured
that I was probably

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.650 align:middle line:84%
wrong in whatever reason I would
have, so I just lowered it.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.010 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK that's fair.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.410 align:middle line:84%
I mean, maybe they don't
want to out themselves,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.410 align:middle line:90%
but I'm kind of curious.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.170 align:middle line:84%
People that guessed very high,
any reasoning about that?

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:00.290 align:middle line:84%
No, maybe they don't
want to say anything.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.570 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's play it one more
time and see what happens.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:06.395 align:middle line:90%


00:04:06.395 --> 00:04:09.110 align:middle line:84%
Ah, so quite
different this time.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:15.618 align:middle line:84%
So now the average was 21 and
the winning choice was 14.

00:04:15.618 --> 00:04:16.410 align:middle line:90%
That's interesting.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:17.970 align:middle line:90%
So this happens every year.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.670 align:middle line:90%
So people are very consistent.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.850 align:middle line:84%
So the distribution
has changed a bit.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.550 align:middle line:84%
So some people must have
decided to bid less this time.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:29.790 align:middle line:84%
Anyone who bid less this time,
do they want to share why?

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.150 align:middle line:90%
Very quiet.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.478 align:middle line:90%
Anyone?

00:04:33.478 --> 00:04:37.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's basically tho
idea that you would keep getting

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:41.990 align:middle line:84%
2/3 and 2/3 and 2/3 of what you
previously thought like would be

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:42.830 align:middle line:90%
a good guess.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:43.372 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:04:43.372 --> 00:04:44.455 align:middle line:90%
Can you say a little more?

00:04:44.455 --> 00:04:46.330 align:middle line:84%
So you thought something
was a good guess,

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.705 align:middle line:84%
and now you're
bidding even less.

00:04:47.705 --> 00:04:48.710 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like I
initially thought

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:50.810 align:middle line:90%
something was a good guess.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.030 align:middle line:84%
And then I was like, OK,
but if I think this, then

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:54.530 align:middle line:84%
what if other people
think the same?

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.750 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
give 2/3 of that.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:58.085 align:middle line:90%
But then 2/3 again of that.

00:04:58.085 --> 00:04:59.710 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going
to give 2/3 of that.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:00.950 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:03.390 align:middle line:84%
So what we see is that
interactive decision making

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:04.690 align:middle line:90%
sometimes creates this regress.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:06.810 align:middle line:84%
So first, I have to
think about my number.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:07.950 align:middle line:90%
That's certainly relevant.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:08.990 align:middle line:84%
Then I have to try
to think about what

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:10.312 align:middle line:90%
other people are going to do.

00:05:10.312 --> 00:05:12.270 align:middle line:84%
So I have to think about
what number they have.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.670 align:middle line:84%
But now I have to put myself in
the shoes of those other people

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:15.950 align:middle line:90%
and say, what are they bidding?

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.158 align:middle line:90%
What are they going to guess?

00:05:17.158 --> 00:05:19.510 align:middle line:84%
Well, they're thinking
about what I'm doing.

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:20.970 align:middle line:84%
But when they try to think
about what I'm doing,

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.810 align:middle line:84%
they have to think about what I
think about what they're doing.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:23.510 align:middle line:84%
So I have think
about what would they

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:24.770 align:middle line:84%
think about what I
think what they think.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:26.310 align:middle line:84%
And all of a sudden, it
gets very, very hard.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.875 align:middle line:84%
And this is exactly what game
theory is trying to address.

00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:30.250 align:middle line:84%
I think for a
while historically,

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.330 align:middle line:84%
if you go back to the
history of thought,

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.770 align:middle line:84%
people thought, it's
just an infinite regress.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.210 align:middle line:84%
There's no way we can try
to analyze games like this.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.990 align:middle line:84%
And game theory is
a formal way to try

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.470 align:middle line:84%
to think about this kind
of reasoning process.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.150 align:middle line:90%
This also happens every year.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.390 align:middle line:84%
A lot of people shift to
100 after we talk about it.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.137 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:05:47.137 --> 00:05:48.970 align:middle line:84%
I think some people are
kind of mischievous.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.345 align:middle line:84%
They want to try to shift
the results and get it wrong.

00:05:51.345 --> 00:05:53.470 align:middle line:84%
So I think this highlights
a really important thing

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.512 align:middle line:84%
about game theory, that
when we study game theory

00:05:55.512 --> 00:05:58.590 align:middle line:84%
and when we analyze a game,
a really important modeling

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:02.030 align:middle line:84%
choice is writing down what
people's preferences actually

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:03.572 align:middle line:90%
are, what people want.

00:06:03.572 --> 00:06:06.030 align:middle line:84%
So if you analyze this game
assuming that people's goal was

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.470 align:middle line:84%
to win, your predictions
might be wrong

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:10.510 align:middle line:84%
if some people's goal
is not actually to win.

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:11.968 align:middle line:84%
So I think some
people's goal might

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:14.170 align:middle line:84%
be to make my prediction
look a little silly

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:16.650 align:middle line:84%
or to make their friends
laugh or to mess with things.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:17.690 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:18.890 align:middle line:90%
That's their preference.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.870 align:middle line:84%
But we need to make
sure we accurately

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:23.090 align:middle line:84%
capture people's preferences
when we model games.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.590 align:middle line:84%
Because if we don't,
our predictions

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.030 align:middle line:90%
are obviously going to be wrong.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:26.990 align:middle line:84%
Another thing,
though, that we have

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.710 align:middle line:84%
to keep in mind is, especially
when comparing the results we

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:32.730 align:middle line:84%
get in a lab or in a classroom
setting to the real world,

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.225 align:middle line:84%
is that the stakes
really matter.

00:06:34.225 --> 00:06:36.350 align:middle line:84%
So we have a lot of students--
maybe a few students

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:37.517 align:middle line:90%
who are kind of mischievous.

00:06:37.517 --> 00:06:39.897 align:middle line:84%
If you won $10 for
guessing, well, I

00:06:39.897 --> 00:06:42.230 align:middle line:84%
think some of those students
wouldn't be so mischievous.

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:44.330 align:middle line:84%
And if you won
$100,000 for winning,

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.290 align:middle line:84%
I think very few students
would be mischievous.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.830 align:middle line:84%
And I think once we get high
enough, essentially no students

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.190 align:middle line:90%
would be mischievous.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.372 align:middle line:84%
So the stakes and what you
actually get for winning

00:06:52.372 --> 00:06:53.830 align:middle line:84%
are going to affect
the preferences

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.510 align:middle line:90%
that people have in this game.

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:58.090 align:middle line:90%
So let's move on.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.170 align:middle line:84%
The plan is to move on
to the formal analysis,

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:03.773 align:middle line:84%
unless there are any
questions about this game.

00:07:03.773 --> 00:07:06.190 align:middle line:84%
I should say this is sometimes
called the Keynesian beauty

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:06.650 align:middle line:90%
contest.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:08.390 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to google
it or read about it,

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:09.410 align:middle line:90%
that's what it's often called.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:09.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the average
wasn't the average

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:16.490 align:middle line:84%
of numbers randomly assigned
by the game to everyone,

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.250 align:middle line:90%
but the average of the choices?

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:19.250 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:20.630 align:middle line:84%
So with this many
people, the average

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:22.670 align:middle line:84%
of the numbers that people
got is almost certain

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.750 align:middle line:90%
to be very close to 50.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.250 align:middle line:84%
But this average is
the average choice,

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.890 align:middle line:84%
so the average guess
of people in the class.

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:32.750 align:middle line:84%
And that's why when we
move from the first round

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:35.330 align:middle line:84%
to the second round, I didn't
see all the realizations,

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:37.990 align:middle line:84%
but it's likely that the average
number that people received

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.870 align:middle line:84%
was basically the same
in the two rounds.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.700 align:middle line:84%
But the average guess
changed dramatically

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.950 align:middle line:84%
because people behaved
differently in the second round

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.310 align:middle line:90%
after we had this discussion.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:46.685 align:middle line:90%
And we could keep doing this.

00:07:46.685 --> 00:07:47.810 align:middle line:90%
We have limited class time.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.370 align:middle line:84%
But my guess if we
played this 10 times,

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.453 align:middle line:84%
we might see people getting
very, very close to 0.

00:07:51.453 --> 00:07:55.190 align:middle line:84%
And my guess is then some people
at 100 to mess with things.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:57.370 align:middle line:84%
Was there a question
here or comment?

00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:58.477 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:58.477 --> 00:07:59.810 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I didn't get a number.

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:00.860 align:middle line:90%
It was just guest.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.110 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if there was a
big number assigned to it.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.390 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Oh, maybe I'm--

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:05.010 align:middle line:90%
oh yeah, you're right.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:05.510 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:06.710 align:middle line:90%
There's no number.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:07.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good point.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.530 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting ahead to
the next game we play.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:09.810 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:09.950 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:11.200 align:middle line:90%
You weren't assigned a number.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:12.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, sorry.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.230 align:middle line:90%
That was very confusing.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.850 align:middle line:84%
We play another game where you
get a number, and fair enough.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:18.058 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you don't get a number.

00:08:18.058 --> 00:08:19.020 align:middle line:90%
There's no uniformity.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:19.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, fair enough.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.470 align:middle line:90%
OK, I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:25.150 align:middle line:90%
Thanks for the clarification.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.530 align:middle line:90%
OK, let us get to the board.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:50.700 align:middle line:90%


00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:51.240 align:middle line:90%
There we go.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.220 align:middle line:84%
So on the problem set, you'll
analyze a variant of this game

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:54.040 align:middle line:90%
where you'll get a number.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:54.960 align:middle line:90%
So that'll be coming.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:10.500 align:middle line:90%


00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:11.680 align:middle line:90%
Let's open this up.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:32.380 align:middle line:90%


00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.820 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's get started.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.340 align:middle line:84%
So first let me say a little
bit more about what we said

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.900 align:middle line:84%
was the definition
of game theory

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:52.020 align:middle line:84%
which we said was the study of
interacting rational decision

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:52.680 align:middle line:90%
makers.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:10:03.300 align:middle line:90%


00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.860 align:middle line:84%
So let's break down each of
the terms in this definition.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.180 align:middle line:84%
Let's go backwards from
easiest to hardest.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.220 align:middle line:84%
So I think the basic
thing is that we're

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:13.600 align:middle line:90%
studying decision makers.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.520 align:middle line:84%
We're studying people
who make decisions.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.360 align:middle line:84%
And that means people
are making choices.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:19.020 align:middle line:84%
So another good
word for decision

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:20.640 align:middle line:84%
that we'll use a
lot in the course

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.180 align:middle line:84%
is people are making
either choices

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:26.860 align:middle line:84%
or we might also
call it actions.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.060 align:middle line:84%
And game theory is a
very abstract, general

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:32.860 align:middle line:84%
mathematical theory that applies
to a huge range of choices

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:33.820 align:middle line:90%
and actions.

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:35.460 align:middle line:84%
So in the game you
just played, where

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:39.900 align:middle line:84%
you chose a number to guess,
that was an example of a choice.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:42.518 align:middle line:84%
Whether you go to MIT is another
example of a choice or action

00:10:42.518 --> 00:10:44.060 align:middle line:84%
that we could analyze
in game theory.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:49.580 align:middle line:84%
What price a firm sets is
another example of a choice.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:52.500 align:middle line:84%
Which country you move
to, what job you choose,

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.045 align:middle line:84%
how many hours a
week you work, these

00:10:54.045 --> 00:10:56.180 align:middle line:84%
are all these
different questions--

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:58.540 align:middle line:84%
what platform a political
party chooses coming up

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:00.560 align:middle line:84%
to an election, who
someone votes for,

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.260 align:middle line:84%
these are all actions or
choices that people make.

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:05.580 align:middle line:84%
And the generality and
abstractness of game theory

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.163 align:middle line:84%
is what allows it to be applied
to so many different settings.

00:11:08.163 --> 00:11:09.747 align:middle line:84%
And throughout the
course, we're going

00:11:09.747 --> 00:11:11.940 align:middle line:84%
to talk about various
applications of game theory,

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:13.460 align:middle line:84%
mainly within
economics, but also

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.260 align:middle line:84%
to some things beyond economics,
like political science

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:19.380 align:middle line:90%
and political bargaining.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:20.872 align:middle line:90%
What does rational mean?

00:11:20.872 --> 00:11:23.080 align:middle line:84%
So this is certainly a
sticking point of game theory.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.120 align:middle line:84%
If you've mentioned
game theory to someone,

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:26.500 align:middle line:84%
whenever I'm on a
plane and people ask me

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.000 align:middle line:84%
what I do and I say
I'm a game theorist,

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:29.320 align:middle line:90%
I always get the same response.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:30.700 align:middle line:90%
But aren't people not rational?

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.260 align:middle line:84%
You probably don't get asked
about game theory on planes

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:37.260 align:middle line:84%
as much as I do, but
maybe someday you will.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:41.100 align:middle line:84%
And I want to be cautious about
how we interpret rationality.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.780 align:middle line:84%
In some contexts, this
is a very bad assumption.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:46.500 align:middle line:90%
There's no question about that.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:49.060 align:middle line:84%
But in other contexts, I
think it's a better assumption

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:51.100 align:middle line:90%
than people might think.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:53.300 align:middle line:84%
And the reason for
that is that the way

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.763 align:middle line:84%
we use the term rationality
within game theory

00:11:55.763 --> 00:11:57.180 align:middle line:84%
is a bit different
than the way we

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.860 align:middle line:84%
use it colloquially
in everyday language.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:04.360 align:middle line:84%
So what you might often hear
someone on the street say is,

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:07.140 align:middle line:84%
it's so irrational
for this person

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.900 align:middle line:84%
to like this
political candidate.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.380 align:middle line:84%
In our model of
rational choice, we

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:15.060 align:middle line:84%
would never say someone's
preferences are irrational.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.160 align:middle line:84%
If that's what they like,
that's what they like.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:18.660 align:middle line:84%
So rationality, I
think a better way

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:21.520 align:middle line:84%
to think of it is it's
really about consistency.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:24.877 align:middle line:90%


00:12:24.877 --> 00:12:26.460 align:middle line:84%
The agents in the
models that we study

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:29.180 align:middle line:84%
are going to act
towards achieving

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:30.860 align:middle line:90%
some consistent objective.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:32.880 align:middle line:84%
You might disagree with
the objective they have.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:33.860 align:middle line:84%
They might like a
different flavor

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:35.818 align:middle line:84%
of ice cream or a different
political candidate

00:12:35.818 --> 00:12:38.260 align:middle line:84%
or put different weight
on income and leisure.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:39.322 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:12:39.322 --> 00:12:41.280 align:middle line:84%
We don't all have to have
the same preferences,

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.660 align:middle line:84%
but the assumption is
that people consistently

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:48.340 align:middle line:84%
act with an eye towards these
preferences that they do have.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:53.020 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes, there's a phrase
that we say and I like to say is

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:54.880 align:middle line:84%
that preferences
cannot be irrational.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:13:01.820 align:middle line:90%


00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:05.020 align:middle line:84%
What can be rational in the
context of game theory choices

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:06.540 align:middle line:90%
giving preferences.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:09.333 align:middle line:84%
So liking chocolate ice
cream is not irrational.

00:13:09.333 --> 00:13:12.000 align:middle line:84%
Even if you really like vanilla,
if your friend likes chocolate,

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:12.480 align:middle line:90%
that's fine.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.500 align:middle line:84%
But if your friend
likes chocolate

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.100 align:middle line:84%
and they choose
vanilla, we would

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:17.058 align:middle line:84%
call that choice irrational
because they're not

00:13:17.058 --> 00:13:19.140 align:middle line:90%
maximizing their objective.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:23.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, consistency-- now
I've shifted the goalposts.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.700 align:middle line:84%
Instead of trying to
defend rationality

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.340 align:middle line:84%
as a game theory
assumption, I'm now

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.740 align:middle line:90%
trying to mention consistency.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:31.520 align:middle line:84%
Again, sometimes it applies,
sometimes it doesn't.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.900 align:middle line:84%
My guess is your preferences
today as 20-year-olds are very

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:37.570 align:middle line:84%
different from the preferences
you had 18 years ago.

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:40.170 align:middle line:84%
So if we're looking at a model
of people's choices over life,

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.175 align:middle line:84%
this probably isn't a
very good description,

00:13:42.175 --> 00:13:44.050 align:middle line:84%
or at least we might
need to enrich the model

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:44.885 align:middle line:90%
to capture that.

00:13:44.885 --> 00:13:47.010 align:middle line:84%
But if we're looking at
your choices or preferences

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:51.610 align:middle line:84%
today and tomorrow, it probably
is a pretty good assumption.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.003 align:middle line:90%
And then interacting.

00:13:53.003 --> 00:13:55.170 align:middle line:84%
This is the key thing that
distinguishes game theory

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.690 align:middle line:84%
from a lot of other
decision problems

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.350 align:middle line:90%
that we study in economics.

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:01.530 align:middle line:84%
And it's exactly what we
saw in this first game.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:04.630 align:middle line:84%
It's that multiple people
are making decisions.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:05.790 align:middle line:90%
But it's more than that.

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.308 align:middle line:84%
There are a lot of situations
where we all make decisions,

00:14:08.308 --> 00:14:10.350 align:middle line:84%
but there's no interaction
between the decisions.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.392 align:middle line:84%
So we don't really have
to take into account what

00:14:12.392 --> 00:14:13.625 align:middle line:90%
decisions other people make.

00:14:13.625 --> 00:14:15.250 align:middle line:84%
What distinguishes
game theory and what

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:17.050 align:middle line:84%
I mean by interacting
decisions is

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.770 align:middle line:84%
that when I'm choosing what
decision to make myself,

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.850 align:middle line:84%
I have to take into account what
other people are going to do.

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:26.570 align:middle line:84%
Because what other people
do affects my preferences.

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:29.410 align:middle line:84%
So this is often called--
the situation is often called

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:31.150 align:middle line:90%
strategic interdependence.

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:38.850 align:middle line:90%


00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:43.130 align:middle line:84%
What's best for me depends
upon what other people do.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:44.810 align:middle line:84%
So a classic example
where this is used

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:47.450 align:middle line:84%
would be penalty kicks
or tennis serves.

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.530 align:middle line:84%
Should the shooter
shoot left or right?

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:52.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's no inherent
reason to shoot left or right.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.210 align:middle line:84%
But whether they should
shoot left or right

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:56.550 align:middle line:84%
depends upon which way the
goalie is going to dive

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:59.550 align:middle line:84%
or which way the tennis serve
receiver is planning to play.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.952 align:middle line:84%
The best action for one
player depends upon what

00:15:01.952 --> 00:15:02.910 align:middle line:90%
other people are doing.

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:06.090 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be
what we study today.

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:08.010 align:middle line:90%
So that's this course.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:09.850 align:middle line:90%
What about this class today?

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:13.690 align:middle line:84%
We're actually going to drop
interacting and just study

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:15.640 align:middle line:84%
a single rational
decision maker.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.390 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be our
baseline for today.

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.650 align:middle line:84%
Because before we can
study how rational decision

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:22.410 align:middle line:84%
makers interact, we have to
study how rational decision

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:25.077 align:middle line:90%
makers act in isolation.

00:15:25.077 --> 00:15:27.410 align:middle line:84%
So today-- and then the rest
of the course will be about

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:29.863 align:middle line:90%
interaction--

00:15:29.863 --> 00:15:31.530 align:middle line:84%
today we're going to
study what's called

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:35.410 align:middle line:90%
individual decision making.

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:38.730 align:middle line:84%
Of course, up here, we
still have individuals.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.410 align:middle line:84%
But the key is that
they're interacting

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:41.270 align:middle line:90%
with other individuals.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:42.830 align:middle line:84%
So maybe individual
is not the best term,

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:43.910 align:middle line:90%
but this is what's used.

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:46.870 align:middle line:84%
We're going to study
individual decision making.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:50.890 align:middle line:90%


00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:52.890 align:middle line:84%
I want to make one final
comment about the scope

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.570 align:middle line:84%
of game theory, which
is the question of who's

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:57.730 align:middle line:90%
taking these actions.

00:15:57.730 --> 00:16:00.603 align:middle line:84%
We often think of it as an
individual making choices.

00:16:00.603 --> 00:16:02.270 align:middle line:84%
But it can be applied
much more broadly.

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:05.110 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of applications
of game theory to evolution,

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:06.970 align:middle line:84%
where we think of
animals or species

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:08.830 align:middle line:84%
kind of collectively
taking actions.

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.730 align:middle line:84%
We might also think about firms
or organizations, governments

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:14.510 align:middle line:84%
taking actions,
committees taking actions.

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:17.948 align:middle line:84%
And I think now with the rise
of artificial intelligence,

00:16:17.948 --> 00:16:19.490 align:middle line:84%
there's a lot of
interest in thinking

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:21.610 align:middle line:84%
of the agents in
our models as being

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:24.343 align:middle line:84%
an LLM or some algorithm
that's taking an action

00:16:24.343 --> 00:16:25.510 align:middle line:90%
and interacting with people.

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:29.530 align:middle line:84%
So one view that is
kind of becoming common,

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:32.002 align:middle line:84%
we can debate about it, is that
the rationality assumptions

00:16:32.002 --> 00:16:34.210 align:middle line:84%
of game theory become more
useful and more compelling

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:35.810 align:middle line:84%
in a world of
artificial intelligence.

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:37.602 align:middle line:84%
We can debate about
that, but that's a view

00:16:37.602 --> 00:16:39.810 align:middle line:90%
that some people express.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:43.890 align:middle line:84%
OK, so here we are,
individual decision making.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:46.310 align:middle line:84%
Let's start with a
very simple problem--

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:48.930 align:middle line:90%


00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.470 align:middle line:84%
a simple what I might call
decision or choice problem.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:54.810 align:middle line:90%


00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:55.790 align:middle line:90%
Nothing deep here.

00:16:55.790 --> 00:16:57.582 align:middle line:84%
Nothing that interesting
about the problem.

00:16:57.582 --> 00:16:59.730 align:middle line:84%
But I just want to show
how we can set it up.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:02.290 align:middle line:84%
So let's say that you're
choosing between three things.

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:05.450 align:middle line:84%
Let's say you're at a cafe and
it's coffee, espresso, and tea.

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:10.690 align:middle line:90%
So we have three choices--

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:16.690 align:middle line:84%
Coffee which I'll label C,
espresso which I'll label E,

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:19.095 align:middle line:90%
and tea, which I'll label T.

00:17:19.095 --> 00:17:20.470 align:middle line:84%
And let's not
think about prices.

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:22.030 align:middle line:84%
Of course, maybe
coffee is cheaper.

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:23.630 align:middle line:84%
But for now, let's not
think about prices.

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:24.470 align:middle line:90%
You just get a choice.

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:25.345 align:middle line:90%
Your friend's paying.

00:17:25.345 --> 00:17:28.052 align:middle line:84%
You're choosing between
these three things.

00:17:28.052 --> 00:17:30.010 align:middle line:84%
The first component that
we need to think about

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:32.428 align:middle line:84%
in this decision problem is,
what are these preferences?

00:17:32.428 --> 00:17:33.970 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we said
that we're studying

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:37.730 align:middle line:84%
decision makers who consistently
maximize some of preference.

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:40.670 align:middle line:84%
So we need to specify
this agent's preferences.

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:44.912 align:middle line:90%


00:17:44.912 --> 00:17:46.370 align:middle line:84%
And in this case,
their preferences

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:49.730 align:middle line:84%
are going to specify some
ordering among these three

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:50.550 align:middle line:90%
choices.

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:52.850 align:middle line:84%
So a great way to represent
this in this context

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:57.150 align:middle line:84%
would be C better
than E better than T.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:59.650 align:middle line:84%
So these are probably
my preferences.

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:00.810 align:middle line:90%
What does this mean?

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:04.930 align:middle line:84%
It means I prefer drinking
coffee to drinking espresso.

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:07.783 align:middle line:84%
I prefer drinking
espresso to drinking tea.

00:18:07.783 --> 00:18:09.950 align:middle line:84%
And the way I've written
this suggests transitivity,

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:11.970 align:middle line:84%
which you will assume
that if I prefer coffee

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:14.330 align:middle line:84%
to espresso and
espresso to tea, then

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:16.290 align:middle line:84%
I must also prefer
coffee to tea.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:17.790 align:middle line:84%
I won't write that
out explicitly,

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:19.430 align:middle line:90%
but that's implied by this.

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:22.050 align:middle line:84%
And there's an implicit
transitivity assumption

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:25.370 align:middle line:90%
that we're making.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:26.997 align:middle line:84%
But I think this
actually raises kind

00:18:26.997 --> 00:18:28.580 align:middle line:84%
of a difficult
philosophical question.

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.600 align:middle line:84%
What does it mean to
prefer coffee to espresso?

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:32.360 align:middle line:90%
What is a preference?

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:34.240 align:middle line:90%
Philosophers talk about this.

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:36.682 align:middle line:84%
Maybe people who study
cognitive sciences go to the lab

00:18:36.682 --> 00:18:38.640 align:middle line:84%
and they try to measure
people's brain activity

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.240 align:middle line:84%
and say, oh, when
you drink coffee,

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:42.427 align:middle line:90%
a different neuron activates.

00:18:42.427 --> 00:18:44.760 align:middle line:84%
Philosophers will get into
these long debates about what

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:46.040 align:middle line:90%
it means to have a preference.

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:49.280 align:middle line:84%
In economics, we take a
much more operational view

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:50.190 align:middle line:90%
of preferences.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:50.940 align:middle line:90%
What does it mean?

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.340 align:middle line:90%
And it's almost tautological.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:55.000 align:middle line:84%
What does it mean that I
prefer coffee to espresso?

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.360 align:middle line:84%
It means when I'm faced
with a choice between coffee

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:59.067 align:middle line:90%
and espresso, I choose coffee.

00:18:59.067 --> 00:18:59.900 align:middle line:90%
That's all it means.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:03.320 align:middle line:84%
So this is the decision based
view of preferences that's

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:04.760 align:middle line:90%
predominant in economics.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:07.140 align:middle line:84%
So let's say what is
the meaning of this?

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:12.640 align:middle line:90%


00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:15.480 align:middle line:84%
The meaning is that if I'm
given a choice between coffee

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:21.198 align:middle line:90%
and espresso, I choose coffee.

00:19:21.198 --> 00:19:22.740 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to be
too precise here.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.280 align:middle line:84%
We might argue about
strict preferences

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:26.420 align:middle line:90%
versus weak preferences.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.460 align:middle line:84%
And maybe we can be
indifferent between things.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:31.440 align:middle line:84%
But for now, let's just think
about strict preferences.

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:34.060 align:middle line:84%
And preferences are a
pairwise object, right?

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:36.080 align:middle line:84%
So a preference says
for any two things,

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:37.700 align:middle line:90%
which do I prefer between them?

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:40.033 align:middle line:84%
So one way of thinking about
what we mean by preferences

00:19:40.033 --> 00:19:42.080 align:middle line:84%
is we're describing
what an agent would

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:46.373 align:middle line:84%
choose from any pair or any
menu containing two items.

00:19:46.373 --> 00:19:48.040 align:middle line:84%
And then from that
primitive preference,

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:52.240 align:middle line:84%
we can talk about what they
would choose in richer problems.

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:56.880 align:middle line:84%
Now, an issue is that these
little squiggly inequality signs

00:19:56.880 --> 00:19:58.420 align:middle line:90%
are kind of messy to deal with.

00:19:58.420 --> 00:20:00.840 align:middle line:84%
So it often becomes more
convenient in economics

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:03.880 align:middle line:84%
to use a utility
representation of preferences.

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:05.500 align:middle line:84%
So let me go to a
new board for this.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.220 align:middle line:84%
And this didn't
go all the way up.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:08.120 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:08.780 align:middle line:90%
There we go.

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:12.680 align:middle line:90%


00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:14.940 align:middle line:84%
And I'll define the word
ordinal in a second.

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:19.560 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to talk
about an ordinal utility

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:21.620 align:middle line:90%
representation of preferences.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:31.800 align:middle line:90%


00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:32.860 align:middle line:90%
Why do we use this?

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:34.560 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's just more convenient.

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:36.380 align:middle line:84%
Especially when we
have a lot of things,

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:38.920 align:middle line:84%
specifying all my pairwise
preferences between these things

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:40.280 align:middle line:90%
can get kind of cumbersome.

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:43.880 align:middle line:84%
So what we do is we just specify
a function called a utility

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:44.420 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:46.100 align:middle line:84%
So let's give just
an example here.

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:53.760 align:middle line:84%
So maybe my utility function
is u of C equals 5, u of E

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:59.800 align:middle line:90%
equals 4, and u of T equals 1.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.360 align:middle line:84%
This should be one
utility representation

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:03.680 align:middle line:90%
of these preferences.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:06.600 align:middle line:84%
Why do these utilities
represent these preferences?

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.760 align:middle line:90%
Well, do I prefer C to E?

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:11.280 align:middle line:84%
Yes, because the
utility assigned to C

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.880 align:middle line:84%
is higher than the
utility assigned to E.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.680 align:middle line:90%
Do I prefer E to T?

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:18.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, yes, because the
utility assigned to E

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:21.200 align:middle line:84%
is strictly greater than
the utility assigned to T.

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:23.140 align:middle line:84%
And if we have a utility
function like this,

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:26.560 align:middle line:84%
you can check that our induced
preferences are going to satisfy

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:27.580 align:middle line:90%
transitivity as well.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.420 align:middle line:84%
Because if one number
is bigger than another

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:31.587 align:middle line:84%
and that other number is
bigger than a third number,

00:21:31.587 --> 00:21:35.440 align:middle line:84%
then the first number is
bigger than the third number.

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:39.400 align:middle line:84%
But we have an issue here that
this is only a representation.

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:41.780 align:middle line:84%
So I can also write down an
alternative utility function.

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:44.320 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll call this u1.

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:49.300 align:middle line:84%
Let me now write down a utility
function u2 that's this.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:59.692 align:middle line:90%


00:21:59.692 --> 00:22:00.900 align:middle line:90%
What about these preferences?

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:02.442 align:middle line:84%
Are these the same
preferences or are

00:22:02.442 --> 00:22:06.400 align:middle line:84%
these different preferences
than the first utility function?

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:07.760 align:middle line:90%
What do people think?

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:11.285 align:middle line:84%
I don't think there's
really one right answer.

00:22:11.285 --> 00:22:13.160 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In a marginal
sense, it's the same.

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:14.243 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: The same, right?

00:22:14.243 --> 00:22:17.860 align:middle line:84%
So it is true that if this
were my utility representation,

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:22.440 align:middle line:84%
it still says I prefer
C to E, I prefer E to T,

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:26.400 align:middle line:84%
and I prefer C to T. So this is
an alternative representation

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:27.760 align:middle line:90%
of the same preferences.

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:31.620 align:middle line:84%
And this is what we mean by an
ordinal utility representation.

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:34.640 align:middle line:90%
Only the order matters.

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:35.500 align:middle line:90%
Let me say this.

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:46.360 align:middle line:90%


00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:47.820 align:middle line:84%
And because only
the order matters,

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:49.720 align:middle line:90%
that has a few implications.

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:54.280 align:middle line:84%
The first is that there's
many utility representations

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:55.540 align:middle line:90%
of the same preferences.

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:58.320 align:middle line:90%


00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:00.220 align:middle line:90%
Many ordinal representations.

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:12.600 align:middle line:90%


00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:14.760 align:middle line:84%
And a second I think
more subtle point

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.960 align:middle line:84%
is that the units and the
values in the utility function

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:18.860 align:middle line:90%
don't have meaning.

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:27.858 align:middle line:90%


00:23:27.858 --> 00:23:29.900 align:middle line:84%
In fact, what are the
units of utility functions?

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:31.220 align:middle line:84%
They don't really have
a unit that makes sense.

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:33.060 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes we call
the units utils.

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:37.440 align:middle line:90%


00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:40.745 align:middle line:84%
But from a purely
preference standpoint,

00:23:40.745 --> 00:23:42.620 align:middle line:84%
you might look at this
and you might say, oh,

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:44.780 align:middle line:84%
I like C more in this
world than in this world.

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:47.480 align:middle line:84%
But no, the way we think
about ordinal representations

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:50.252 align:middle line:84%
is that what matters
are your preferences.

00:23:50.252 --> 00:23:51.960 align:middle line:84%
And there's a convenient
mathematical way

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:53.722 align:middle line:90%
to represent them like this.

00:23:53.722 --> 00:23:55.680 align:middle line:84%
And this is another
convenient mathematical way

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:56.740 align:middle line:90%
to represent them.

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.660 align:middle line:84%
And they represent
the same preferences.

00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:00.160 align:middle line:84%
And there's no
meaning associated

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:02.740 align:middle line:84%
with these utility functions
in this ordinal world.

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:03.240 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:05.920 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Would you have to
specify a null option, then?

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:09.760 align:middle line:84%
Let's say if the utility
is negative 100 for T,

00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:12.520 align:middle line:84%
would you have to specify that
they could also choose nothing?

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:14.062 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Great,
that's a good point.

00:24:14.062 --> 00:24:16.590 align:middle line:84%
So when we write a choice
problem, it's really crucial--

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:19.257 align:middle line:84%
and it's implicit in what I did,
but I didn't say it explicitly,

00:24:19.257 --> 00:24:22.990 align:middle line:84%
so thanks for bringing this up--
that I can make only one choice,

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:25.343 align:middle line:84%
and every possible
choice is included.

00:24:25.343 --> 00:24:28.010 align:middle line:84%
So if you have four options, if
only three of them are included,

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:30.718 align:middle line:84%
that's not going to capture your
preference problem-- your choice

00:24:30.718 --> 00:24:31.370 align:middle line:90%
problem.

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:33.402 align:middle line:84%
So really, if I
were being careful,

00:24:33.402 --> 00:24:35.610 align:middle line:84%
probably this is actually
a more complicated problem.

00:24:35.610 --> 00:24:38.150 align:middle line:84%
And there's a third option,
which is not drinking any drink.

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:40.410 align:middle line:84%
And I should really
specify the utility here.

00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:42.150 align:middle line:84%
So in this simple
thing I'm imagining

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:44.650 align:middle line:84%
for some reason you have to
make one of these three choices.

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:45.090 align:middle line:90%
But you're right.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:46.965 align:middle line:84%
In general, when we
model a choice problem,

00:24:46.965 --> 00:24:49.590 align:middle line:84%
it's important to capture every
possible choice the agent could

00:24:49.590 --> 00:24:50.270 align:middle line:90%
make.

00:24:50.270 --> 00:24:53.670 align:middle line:84%
On the other side, indeed,
if it's possible for me

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:55.390 align:middle line:84%
to choose both
coffee and espresso,

00:24:55.390 --> 00:24:57.070 align:middle line:84%
then that needs to
be formally modeled

00:24:57.070 --> 00:25:00.330 align:middle line:84%
as another choice, the bundle
where I get coffee and espresso,

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:02.390 align:middle line:84%
because that's a separate
choice that I can make.

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:04.150 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:06.790 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is ordinal
utility representations.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:09.070 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to
move to the opposite,

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:10.170 align:middle line:90%
cardinal representations.

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:12.850 align:middle line:84%
And everything I said here
is going to go the other way.

00:25:12.850 --> 00:25:14.010 align:middle line:90%
So let's be really clear.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:15.190 align:middle line:84%
Everything I just
said is going to be

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:16.410 align:middle line:90%
wrong in a different context.

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:18.910 align:middle line:90%
So let's be very careful.

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:21.430 align:middle line:90%
So this is ordinal.

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:25.290 align:middle line:84%
The issue is that
often in game theory,

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.510 align:middle line:84%
we face decisions
under uncertainty.

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:29.430 align:middle line:84%
And when we face decisions
under uncertainty,

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:32.110 align:middle line:84%
we need to model preferences
a bit differently.

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:34.890 align:middle line:84%
So let's now move to
decisions under uncertainty.

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:41.830 align:middle line:90%


00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:43.910 align:middle line:84%
What is a decision
under uncertainty mean?

00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:46.510 align:middle line:84%
It means when I make
a choice, I don't

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:48.510 align:middle line:84%
know exactly what the
consequence of that choice

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:49.350 align:middle line:90%
will be.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.670 align:middle line:84%
When I buy an index
fund or buy a stock,

00:25:51.670 --> 00:25:54.630 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether that stock
is going to go up or down.

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.350 align:middle line:84%
I can't choose-- of
course, if I could

00:25:56.350 --> 00:25:58.610 align:middle line:84%
choose the stock that made more
money, I would choose that one.

00:25:58.610 --> 00:25:59.652 align:middle line:90%
But that's not my choice.

00:25:59.652 --> 00:26:02.377 align:middle line:84%
I'm choosing one stock that has
some distribution over outcomes

00:26:02.377 --> 00:26:04.210 align:middle line:84%
that I don't know what
the outcomes will be.

00:26:04.210 --> 00:26:05.570 align:middle line:84%
And then I might
choose another stock.

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:08.230 align:middle line:84%
And again, I don't know exactly
what the outcome of that stock

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:08.890 align:middle line:90%
will be.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.270 align:middle line:84%
And that's what we
mean by uncertainty.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:14.333 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at, again, a
very, very simple problem, not

00:26:14.333 --> 00:26:15.750 align:middle line:84%
economically
interesting, but just

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.633 align:middle line:84%
to convey the ideas, which is
the choice of how to get home.

00:26:18.633 --> 00:26:20.550 align:middle line:84%
Let's say you're choosing
whether to walk home

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:21.910 align:middle line:90%
or to take the subway.

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:27.670 align:middle line:84%
So you can either walk
home or take the subway.

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:29.763 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we'll call that
and we'll call this W.

00:26:29.763 --> 00:26:31.930 align:middle line:84%
And again, here we're
assuming you have to get home.

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:33.810 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's a third option
where you stay at work all day.

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:35.690 align:middle line:84%
But we're not going
to model that.

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:41.528 align:middle line:90%


00:26:41.528 --> 00:26:43.570 align:middle line:84%
And now why is this a
decision under uncertainty?

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:45.130 align:middle line:84%
Well, you don't know what the
weather is going to be like.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:46.838 align:middle line:84%
You have a long walk
home, so the weather

00:26:46.838 --> 00:26:48.190 align:middle line:90%
could change as you walk home.

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:50.607 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to assume
there's two ways the world could

00:26:50.607 --> 00:26:51.130 align:middle line:90%
be again.

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:52.350 align:middle line:84%
Weather it's more
complicated, but this

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:54.010 align:middle line:90%
is a simplification of reality.

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:55.970 align:middle line:84%
It could be sunny or
it could be rainy.

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:58.338 align:middle line:90%


00:26:58.338 --> 00:27:00.630 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we should really model
every possible temperature

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:02.530 align:middle line:84%
and every possible
level of precipitation,

00:27:02.530 --> 00:27:04.350 align:middle line:90%
but we're not going to do that.

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:08.410 align:middle line:84%
And the way things work
is when it's sunny,

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:11.570 align:middle line:84%
you'd rather walk home
and when it's rainy,

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:13.630 align:middle line:90%
you'd rather take the subway.

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:15.830 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll write
this over here.

00:27:15.830 --> 00:27:22.430 align:middle line:90%
If sunny, you prefer--

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:25.590 align:middle line:84%
and I think sometimes I call
this the T to avoid the S.

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:32.470 align:middle line:84%
Let's call this the T. Maybe
TA, just so we don't see the two

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:33.670 align:middle line:90%
S's.

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:35.190 align:middle line:90%
Sunny, you prefer to walk home.

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:42.345 align:middle line:84%
And if rainy, you
prefer to take the T.

00:27:42.345 --> 00:27:43.970 align:middle line:84%
So how would you
approach this problem?

00:27:43.970 --> 00:27:46.470 align:middle line:84%
And this is a very
simple problem,

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:48.843 align:middle line:84%
a simple choice people
make all the time.

00:27:48.843 --> 00:27:50.010 align:middle line:90%
How would you approach this?

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:53.670 align:middle line:90%


00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:57.670 align:middle line:84%
What do you do when you-- do
you just randomly go home or do

00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:00.630 align:middle line:90%
you think a little bit?

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:01.370 align:middle line:90%
What's relevant?

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:03.910 align:middle line:84%
What do you need to know
to approach this problem?

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:04.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:05.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You check
the weather app.

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:07.110 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: You check
the weather app.

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:08.277 align:middle line:90%
And what does that tell you?

00:28:08.277 --> 00:28:09.652 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If it's
sunny or rainy.

00:28:09.652 --> 00:28:10.230 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:12.328 align:middle line:84%
And it might tell
you-- and really, it

00:28:12.328 --> 00:28:14.370 align:middle line:84%
might give you some-- or
do you want to go ahead?

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:14.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:15.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It'd be
how likely it is.

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:16.110 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:17.730 align:middle line:84%
It's going to tell you--
it might say sun or rain,

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:19.610 align:middle line:84%
but also it might give you
a likelihood of sun or rain.

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:21.032 align:middle line:84%
And oftentimes,
you're not certain

00:28:21.032 --> 00:28:22.490 align:middle line:84%
if it's going to
be sunny or rainy.

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:23.573 align:middle line:90%
You have some uncertainty.

00:28:23.573 --> 00:28:25.450 align:middle line:84%
That's the key thing
that we're having here.

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:28.088 align:middle line:84%
So this problem, if you knew
the weather, would be very easy.

00:28:28.088 --> 00:28:30.130 align:middle line:84%
If you knew for certain
it was going to be sunny,

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:31.310 align:middle line:90%
we know you'd walk home.

00:28:31.310 --> 00:28:32.890 align:middle line:84%
And if you knew for certain
it was going to rain,

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:33.670 align:middle line:90%
you'd take the T.

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:35.418 align:middle line:84%
The issue is that
you have uncertainty.

00:28:35.418 --> 00:28:37.710 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, when you check
the weather app, what you do

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:38.930 align:middle line:90%
is you form beliefs.

00:28:38.930 --> 00:28:41.550 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be
kind of a foundational idea

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:53.810 align:middle line:84%
in this course, that in the face
of uncertainty, what do you do?

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:54.770 align:middle line:90%
You form beliefs.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:57.350 align:middle line:90%


00:28:57.350 --> 00:29:00.070 align:middle line:84%
So in game theory, we take a
fundamentally Bayesian view

00:29:00.070 --> 00:29:00.890 align:middle line:90%
of the world.

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:02.290 align:middle line:84%
When we have
uncertainty, we say,

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:04.930 align:middle line:84%
well, we don't know whether
it's going to be sunny or rainy.

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:06.597 align:middle line:84%
But we might say
something like, there's

00:29:06.597 --> 00:29:08.260 align:middle line:90%
a 20% chance that it's sunny.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:09.600 align:middle line:90%
So we'll assign beliefs.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:12.300 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, let's
represent our belief

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:15.180 align:middle line:84%
by the probability
p that it's sunny.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:18.500 align:middle line:84%
And then correspondingly, we
have the probability 1 minus

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:21.100 align:middle line:90%
p that it's raining.

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:23.220 align:middle line:90%
So these are my beliefs.

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:23.760 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:27.180 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to fill
in some utilities here.

00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:35.740 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to say 7, 2, 5, 5.

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:38.080 align:middle line:84%
So this is a representation
of my preferences.

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:41.180 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, when it's sunny, I
prefer walking to taking the T.

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:46.620 align:middle line:84%
And when it's rainy, I prefer
taking the T to walking.

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:48.820 align:middle line:84%
So now we've
written things down.

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:49.680 align:middle line:90%
Now I'd ask again.

00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:52.220 align:middle line:84%
Now we've specified our
beliefs, which would you choose?

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:55.066 align:middle line:84%
How would you
approach this choice?

00:29:55.066 --> 00:29:56.683 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Find an
expected utility.

00:29:56.683 --> 00:29:58.100 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So in
this course, we're

00:29:58.100 --> 00:29:59.560 align:middle line:84%
going to assume
expected utility.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:01.840 align:middle line:84%
But I want to point out
that that's not so obvious.

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:03.760 align:middle line:84%
One thing you might say
you might say, look,

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:06.460 align:middle line:90%
I'm an optimistic person.

00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:10.180 align:middle line:84%
If I walk, the best thing
that can happen is I get 7.

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:12.820 align:middle line:84%
If I take the T, the best thing
that can happen is I get 5.

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:14.120 align:middle line:90%
7 is higher than 5.

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:15.020 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to walk.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:16.493 align:middle line:90%
I'm an optimistic person.

00:30:16.493 --> 00:30:18.160 align:middle line:84%
You might say, I'm a
pessimistic person.

00:30:18.160 --> 00:30:21.060 align:middle line:84%
I'm really afraid of
the worst-case outcome.

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:23.340 align:middle line:90%
So when I walk, I could get 2.

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:25.560 align:middle line:84%
When I take the T, the
worst I can get is 5.

00:30:25.560 --> 00:30:27.763 align:middle line:84%
That's better, so I'm
going to take the T.

00:30:27.763 --> 00:30:29.180 align:middle line:84%
I think the view
in game theory is

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:31.540 align:middle line:84%
that optimism and
pessimism are better

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:33.740 align:middle line:90%
reflected in your beliefs.

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:36.980 align:middle line:84%
And we're generally going to
assume in this course expected

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:37.640 align:middle line:90%
utility.

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:42.660 align:middle line:90%


00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:44.900 align:middle line:84%
But I want to point out
that this is an assumption.

00:30:44.900 --> 00:30:46.760 align:middle line:90%
There's an axiomatic foundation.

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:49.220 align:middle line:84%
So decision theorists think
a lot about the question,

00:30:49.220 --> 00:30:51.340 align:middle line:84%
why should we use
expected utility?

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:53.860 align:middle line:84%
Or more concretely, if
we assume that agents

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:56.753 align:middle line:84%
use expected utility, what
assumption are we making?

00:30:56.753 --> 00:30:58.420 align:middle line:84%
What does that mean
about their behavior

00:30:58.420 --> 00:30:59.753 align:middle line:90%
when we assume expected utility?

00:30:59.753 --> 00:31:01.300 align:middle line:84%
How restrictive is
that assumption?

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:03.283 align:middle line:84%
And there's an appendix
in the lecture notes

00:31:03.283 --> 00:31:04.700 align:middle line:84%
that's posted
online where you can

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:07.840 align:middle line:84%
look at this axiomatic approach
to expected utility theory.

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:09.340 align:middle line:84%
But in this course,
we're just going

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:12.400 align:middle line:84%
to take it as given that people
always use expected utility.

00:31:12.400 --> 00:31:13.400 align:middle line:90%
So what does that mean?

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:16.020 align:middle line:90%
It's two steps.

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:17.520 align:middle line:90%
First, they form beliefs.

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:27.123 align:middle line:90%


00:31:27.123 --> 00:31:28.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, how reasonable
is it to think

00:31:28.540 --> 00:31:30.260 align:middle line:84%
that people form
beliefs about the thing

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:31.440 align:middle line:90%
they're uncertain about?

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:33.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, I think it
depends on the context.

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:35.557 align:middle line:84%
In this problem, I think
it's very reasonable.

00:31:35.557 --> 00:31:36.640 align:middle line:90%
You open your weather app.

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:39.040 align:middle line:84%
It gives you some
probability of rain or sun.

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:40.680 align:middle line:84%
You might think, oh, I don't
really trust the weather app.

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:42.200 align:middle line:84%
It always overestimates
or underestimates.

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:42.908 align:middle line:90%
You might adjust.

00:31:42.908 --> 00:31:44.300 align:middle line:90%
But you'll form your belief.

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:49.140 align:middle line:84%
But if I said, form
your belief about what

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:51.540 align:middle line:84%
is the probability
that in 15 years

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:54.460 align:middle line:84%
your income will be
between these two numbers.

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:56.402 align:middle line:84%
That's pretty hard to
form beliefs about.

00:31:56.402 --> 00:31:57.860 align:middle line:84%
So I think this
assumption that you

00:31:57.860 --> 00:32:00.020 align:middle line:84%
start by forming beliefs
about what you don't know

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:03.120 align:middle line:84%
is a very realistic assumption
in very simple problems.

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:05.500 align:middle line:84%
But as we get to very, very
rich problems and complicated

00:32:05.500 --> 00:32:08.300 align:middle line:84%
problems, you might
worry that this is not

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:09.680 align:middle line:90%
a very well-founded assumption.

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:11.600 align:middle line:84%
When we get to
complicated choices,

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:15.180 align:middle line:84%
maybe your choice about
what job to take today

00:32:15.180 --> 00:32:16.780 align:middle line:84%
depend on your belief
about how likely

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:20.380 align:middle line:84%
it is that you have a grandchild
in 40 years who's short on money

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:23.840 align:middle line:84%
and needs the money that you
saved based on your job today.

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:24.800 align:middle line:90%
Or let's go deeper.

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:26.740 align:middle line:84%
What's the probability
that in 10 generations,

00:32:26.740 --> 00:32:31.020 align:middle line:84%
your 10th degree grandchild
is going to be short on money?

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:32.980 align:middle line:84%
Wow, that's really hard
to form beliefs about.

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:35.480 align:middle line:84%
So in that context, maybe this
is an unrealistic assumption,

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:36.500 align:middle line:84%
but it's going to
be the assumption we

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:37.200 align:middle line:90%
make in this class.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:39.450 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to apply
this to models where we think

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:40.740 align:middle line:90%
it's a reasonable assumption.

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:42.940 align:middle line:84%
And then the next
thing that you do,

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:44.440 align:middle line:84%
given that you
formed your beliefs,

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:46.880 align:middle line:84%
is you compute your expected
utility from each choice.

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:55.980 align:middle line:90%


00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:57.620 align:middle line:84%
But now we have a
bit of an issue.

00:32:57.620 --> 00:33:00.980 align:middle line:84%
Because I set up here when I
talked about ordinal utility

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:04.338 align:middle line:84%
representations that the
numbers didn't matter.

00:33:04.338 --> 00:33:06.380 align:middle line:84%
But now all of a sudden,
the numbers will matter.

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:09.620 align:middle line:84%
Because when I compute the
expectation, my expected utility

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:12.940 align:middle line:84%
from walking, if
7 were 700, that's

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:15.500 align:middle line:84%
going to change my
expected utility.

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:19.140 align:middle line:84%
So we have to really crucially
understand that in this model,

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:20.880 align:middle line:90%
these utilities are different.

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:23.300 align:middle line:84%
These are no longer
ordinal utilities.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:28.420 align:middle line:84%
So in the decision making
under uncertainty--

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:43.220 align:middle line:84%
so in this context, utility is
cardinal as opposed to ordinal.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:46.000 align:middle line:84%
Ordinal means only
the order matters.

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:48.240 align:middle line:84%
That's where the word
ordinal comes from.

00:33:48.240 --> 00:33:52.020 align:middle line:84%
Cardinal means the size or
the actual numbers matter.

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:55.300 align:middle line:84%
And the way we
distinguish this is we

00:33:55.300 --> 00:33:57.180 align:middle line:84%
say that this
utility function here

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:59.830 align:middle line:84%
is a Von Neumann-Morgenstern
utility function.

00:33:59.830 --> 00:34:12.929 align:middle line:90%


00:34:12.929 --> 00:34:14.750 align:middle line:84%
And basically,
throughout this course,

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:17.130 align:middle line:84%
we're going to focus on
this case of uncertainty.

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:20.090 align:middle line:84%
And essentially, all the
utility functions we work with

00:34:20.090 --> 00:34:23.810 align:middle line:84%
are going to be cardinal utility
functions or Von Neumann utility

00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:26.630 align:middle line:84%
functions rather than
ordinal utility functions.

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:29.290 align:middle line:84%
And just to understand
the landscape,

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:33.190 align:middle line:84%
up here, we didn't make any
expected utility assumptions.

00:34:33.190 --> 00:34:34.889 align:middle line:84%
This was a very
abstract approach

00:34:34.889 --> 00:34:36.650 align:middle line:90%
to any preference problem.

00:34:36.650 --> 00:34:38.370 align:middle line:84%
When we're in the
world of uncertainty

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:41.889 align:middle line:84%
and we restrict attention to
expected utility preferences,

00:34:41.889 --> 00:34:43.550 align:middle line:84%
in that more
restricted environment,

00:34:43.550 --> 00:34:45.050 align:middle line:84%
we're always going
to work with what

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:46.889 align:middle line:84%
are called Von
Neumann-Morgenstern utility

00:34:46.889 --> 00:34:51.610 align:middle line:84%
functions, where the numerical
value actually matters.

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.010 align:middle line:84%
So let's see how we would
approach this problem

00:34:54.010 --> 00:34:56.210 align:middle line:90%
in this VNM context.

00:34:56.210 --> 00:34:58.330 align:middle line:90%
So we have two choices.

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:03.432 align:middle line:84%
We have walking
and taking the T.

00:35:03.432 --> 00:35:04.890 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm
generally going to use

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:07.930 align:middle line:84%
is this Von Neumann-Morgenstern
utility function u.

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:11.330 align:middle line:90%
This is a small u.

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:13.670 align:middle line:84%
But then when I compute
expected utilities,

00:35:13.670 --> 00:35:15.750 align:middle line:84%
your expected utility
is going to be a big U.

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:19.530 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to now
compute here U of walking

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:22.690 align:middle line:84%
and U of taken the T.
And this is a big U.

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:25.190 align:middle line:84%
And of course with handwriting,
it's not obvious what it is.

00:35:25.190 --> 00:35:27.315 align:middle line:84%
So if you're ever
unsure, you can ask me.

00:35:27.315 --> 00:35:28.690 align:middle line:84%
But let's make
sure we understand

00:35:28.690 --> 00:35:32.050 align:middle line:84%
that these functions are
defined for different objects.

00:35:32.050 --> 00:35:35.370 align:middle line:84%
My cardinal utility
tells me what

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:38.130 align:middle line:84%
is my utility from
walking when it's sunny?

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:40.310 align:middle line:84%
What is my utility from
walking when it's rainy?

00:35:40.310 --> 00:35:42.450 align:middle line:84%
And it gives me
these four numbers.

00:35:42.450 --> 00:35:46.930 align:middle line:84%
My big U, my expected
utility, isn't assigned

00:35:46.930 --> 00:35:49.850 align:middle line:90%
to a weather-choice pair.

00:35:49.850 --> 00:35:52.130 align:middle line:84%
It's assigned just
to the choice.

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:55.570 align:middle line:84%
So for my big U, I can say,
what is my expected utility

00:35:55.570 --> 00:35:57.250 align:middle line:90%
from walking?

00:35:57.250 --> 00:35:59.170 align:middle line:84%
My little you
tells me my utility

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:01.450 align:middle line:84%
from walking in each
state of the world.

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:02.750 align:middle line:90%
So let's just compute this.

00:36:02.750 --> 00:36:04.378 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be quite easy.

00:36:04.378 --> 00:36:05.670 align:middle line:90%
The math here is not the point.

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:07.850 align:middle line:90%
It's about conceptually.

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:10.970 align:middle line:84%
So if I walk, well, with
probability p I get 7

00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:13.170 align:middle line:84%
and with probability
1 minus p I get 2.

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:21.330 align:middle line:84%
So my expected utility is p
times 7 plus 1 minus p times 2.

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:26.410 align:middle line:84%
And if I take the T,
well, it's p times 5

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:30.627 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 minus p times 5.

00:36:30.627 --> 00:36:31.710 align:middle line:90%
Well, this is easy, right?

00:36:31.710 --> 00:36:33.990 align:middle line:84%
The expectation of this
is just going to be 5.

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:35.170 align:middle line:90%
It's 5 either way.

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:38.210 align:middle line:90%
So this is 5.

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:41.970 align:middle line:90%
And then here what do I get?

00:36:41.970 --> 00:36:42.690 align:middle line:90%
I get 2.

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:45.090 align:middle line:90%
Minus 2p, so plus 5p.

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:48.850 align:middle line:90%


00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:50.690 align:middle line:90%
So which do I prefer?

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:52.570 align:middle line:90%
Well, it depends on p, right?

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:56.130 align:middle line:84%
If p is really, really
small-- if p gets close to 0,

00:36:56.130 --> 00:36:57.810 align:middle line:90%
then this becomes 2.

00:36:57.810 --> 00:36:59.370 align:middle line:90%
And I prefer 5 to 2.

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:01.290 align:middle line:90%
So I prefer taking the T.

00:37:01.290 --> 00:37:02.203 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:37:02.203 --> 00:37:04.370 align:middle line:84%
If I look outside and it
looks really, really rainy,

00:37:04.370 --> 00:37:07.930 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to take the T. If p
is really, really high-- well,

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:09.070 align:middle line:90%
let's say p is 1.

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:10.650 align:middle line:90%
I know it's going to be sunny.

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:13.930 align:middle line:90%
Then if I walk, I get 7.

00:37:13.930 --> 00:37:15.430 align:middle line:90%
Yes, if I walk, I get 7.

00:37:15.430 --> 00:37:17.330 align:middle line:90%
If I take the T, I get 5.

00:37:17.330 --> 00:37:19.170 align:middle line:90%
7 is more than 5.

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:22.370 align:middle line:90%
So therefore, I prefer to walk.

00:37:22.370 --> 00:37:24.070 align:middle line:84%
When will I walk
rather than take the T?

00:37:24.070 --> 00:37:25.910 align:middle line:84%
Well, I have to compare
these two numbers.

00:37:25.910 --> 00:37:29.510 align:middle line:84%
So when is 2 plus 5p
greater than or equal to 5?

00:37:29.510 --> 00:37:30.630 align:middle line:90%
Let's ask that question.

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:37.010 align:middle line:90%


00:37:37.010 --> 00:37:40.410 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's if 5p is
greater than or equal to 3.

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:46.370 align:middle line:84%
So if p is greater than
or equal to 3/5, right?

00:37:46.370 --> 00:37:46.870 align:middle line:90%
All right?

00:37:46.870 --> 00:37:48.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:37:48.410 --> 00:37:50.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure someone
will check the math.

00:37:50.990 --> 00:37:54.330 align:middle line:90%


00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:56.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:37:56.090 --> 00:37:59.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why is it
greater than 3/5?

00:37:59.010 --> 00:38:00.230 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Ah, good point.

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:04.090 align:middle line:84%
So when p is exactly 3/5, I'm
indifferent between the two.

00:38:04.090 --> 00:38:05.710 align:middle line:90%
So I need some word for that.

00:38:05.710 --> 00:38:10.210 align:middle line:84%
So I guess I would say I weakly
prefer walking to taking the T

00:38:10.210 --> 00:38:12.250 align:middle line:84%
if p is greater than
or equal to 3/5.

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:17.010 align:middle line:84%
And I strictly prefer walking
to taking the T if p is strictly

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:18.730 align:middle line:90%
greater than 3/5.

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:20.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But wouldn't
that make sense

00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:25.690 align:middle line:84%
that the equal part would be
for the T, not for walking?

00:38:25.690 --> 00:38:27.290 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Sorry, say it again.

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:31.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So since it's
greater or equal, when

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:32.380 align:middle line:90%
this happens, we walk, right?

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:33.130 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:33.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:38:33.850 --> 00:38:37.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So why are we
putting equal in walking?

00:38:37.810 --> 00:38:40.770 align:middle line:84%
Is there like-- do we
say like when it's equal,

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:41.510 align:middle line:90%
it's indifferent?

00:38:41.510 --> 00:38:43.232 align:middle line:90%
Do we decide which one?

00:38:43.232 --> 00:38:44.190 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You're right.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:45.690 align:middle line:84%
So there's a bit
of ambiguity there.

00:38:45.690 --> 00:38:48.330 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll put-- if this
makes it-- let's say this, yeah.

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:51.030 align:middle line:84%
I would say, let's focus on
the case where p is not 3/5.

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:52.050 align:middle line:90%
Then it's pretty clear.

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:54.110 align:middle line:84%
When p is exactly
3/5, you're right,

00:38:54.110 --> 00:38:56.530 align:middle line:84%
we wouldn't have an inherent
preference for walking over

00:38:56.530 --> 00:38:59.370 align:middle line:84%
taking the T. In that case, we
would be indifferent between

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:00.510 align:middle line:90%
walking and taking the T.

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:03.157 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll make a note
up here and sometimes use

00:39:03.157 --> 00:39:03.990 align:middle line:90%
a squiggle for that.

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:08.570 align:middle line:84%
So we might say W squiggle T,
I'm indifferent between walking

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:17.290 align:middle line:84%
and taking the T,
exactly if p equals 3/5.

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:19.108 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, it's
a strict preference.

00:39:19.108 --> 00:39:20.650 align:middle line:84%
So here what this
says, which I think

00:39:20.650 --> 00:39:22.810 align:middle line:84%
is an intuitive model
of decision making,

00:39:22.810 --> 00:39:25.970 align:middle line:84%
that I'm going to choose to walk
home when I check my weather app

00:39:25.970 --> 00:39:28.610 align:middle line:84%
and I think it's sufficiently
likely that it's sunny.

00:39:28.610 --> 00:39:29.910 align:middle line:90%
How sufficiently likely?

00:39:29.910 --> 00:39:32.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, in this case, it's
exactly 60% chance of sun

00:39:32.670 --> 00:39:35.330 align:middle line:84%
that makes it worth it
for me to walk home.

00:39:35.330 --> 00:39:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Now, where did we
get this number from?

00:39:37.690 --> 00:39:41.402 align:middle line:84%
It depended on the
numerical values over here.

00:39:41.402 --> 00:39:43.110 align:middle line:84%
If these numerical
values were different,

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:45.750 align:middle line:84%
if I made this 700, well, then
I'm basically always going

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:48.000 align:middle line:84%
to walk home unless I'm
absolutely certain, basically,

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:49.040 align:middle line:90%
it's going to rain.

00:39:49.040 --> 00:39:53.360 align:middle line:84%
So that again shows us that the
cardinal values of these utility

00:39:53.360 --> 00:39:56.440 align:middle line:84%
functions are going to affect
the decisions that we make.

00:39:56.440 --> 00:39:57.700 align:middle line:90%
Any other question on this?

00:39:57.700 --> 00:39:58.412 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:39:58.412 --> 00:40:00.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in the
case when it's equal,

00:40:00.120 --> 00:40:03.700 align:middle line:84%
is there-- like, now we
use expected utility.

00:40:03.700 --> 00:40:07.000 align:middle line:84%
So you can see which
expected utility is bigger.

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:10.240 align:middle line:84%
But in the case it's equal, is
there a rule we should follow?

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:12.960 align:middle line:84%
Or based on the numbers
we have in the grid--

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:15.173 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: No, I would
say the right way

00:40:15.173 --> 00:40:16.340 align:middle line:90%
to think about preferences--

00:40:16.340 --> 00:40:18.940 align:middle line:84%
I was being a little loose
over there to make it quicker.

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:21.333 align:middle line:84%
But I'd say really,
there's three possibilities

00:40:21.333 --> 00:40:22.500 align:middle line:90%
when you compare two things.

00:40:22.500 --> 00:40:24.208 align:middle line:84%
You can strictly prefer
one to the other.

00:40:24.208 --> 00:40:26.737 align:middle line:84%
You can strictly prefer
the other to the first one.

00:40:26.737 --> 00:40:27.820 align:middle line:90%
Or you can be indifferent.

00:40:27.820 --> 00:40:29.278 align:middle line:84%
There's really
three possibilities.

00:40:29.278 --> 00:40:31.043 align:middle line:84%
And when I compare my
expected utilities,

00:40:31.043 --> 00:40:32.460 align:middle line:84%
there's again three
possibilities.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:34.780 align:middle line:84%
Either one is strictly higher,
the other is strictly higher,

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:36.100 align:middle line:90%
or we're exactly indifferent.

00:40:36.100 --> 00:40:38.650 align:middle line:84%
And in general, when
people are indifferent,

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:40.900 align:middle line:84%
it's harder to make predictions
about what they'll do.

00:40:40.900 --> 00:40:41.700 align:middle line:90%
They might randomize.

00:40:41.700 --> 00:40:42.380 align:middle line:90%
They might choose one.

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:43.100 align:middle line:90%
They might choose the other.

00:40:43.100 --> 00:40:44.600 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to really
take a strong stance on what

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:45.743 align:middle line:90%
people do in that case.

00:40:45.743 --> 00:40:46.660 align:middle line:90%
Thanks for clarifying.

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:47.160 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:50.320 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:40:50.320 --> 00:40:50.900 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:52.600 align:middle line:84%
So now let's be a
bit more formal.

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:57.100 align:middle line:84%
This was a very simple example,
but let's formalize this a bit.

00:40:57.100 --> 00:40:58.460 align:middle line:90%
I guess I have one more board.

00:40:58.460 --> 00:41:07.000 align:middle line:90%


00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:09.400 align:middle line:90%
So maybe I'll say VNM.

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:11.980 align:middle line:84%
This is Von Neumann-Morgenstern
that I said before.

00:41:11.980 --> 00:41:14.100 align:middle line:84%
And I'll call this
the VNM setting.

00:41:14.100 --> 00:41:17.423 align:middle line:90%


00:41:17.423 --> 00:41:18.840 align:middle line:84%
So generally, this
is the approach

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:20.220 align:middle line:84%
we're going to take
in this course.

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:22.262 align:middle line:84%
We're going to start with
a really simple example

00:41:22.262 --> 00:41:23.400 align:middle line:90%
to motivate the theory.

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:25.905 align:middle line:84%
We're then going to present a
more general abstract model.

00:41:25.905 --> 00:41:28.280 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to go
back to more concrete and more

00:41:28.280 --> 00:41:30.020 align:middle line:84%
interesting applications
that are more substantive.

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:31.580 align:middle line:84%
So that's the three
steps we'll take.

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:32.840 align:middle line:90%
So what's the setting here?

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:36.600 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's going to be a
set Z, which we'll say is the--

00:41:36.600 --> 00:41:41.480 align:middle line:84%
and I'll call this
Z1 through Zm.

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:46.980 align:middle line:84%
And this is the set of maybe
outcomes or consequences.

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:49.513 align:middle line:90%


00:41:49.513 --> 00:41:51.680 align:middle line:84%
And we have to be really
careful about how we define

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:53.080 align:middle line:90%
outcomes and consequences.

00:41:53.080 --> 00:41:55.440 align:middle line:84%
So in this game
that we described,

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:57.880 align:middle line:84%
there are actually
four outcomes--

00:41:57.880 --> 00:42:00.880 align:middle line:84%
I walk home in the sun,
I walk home in the rain,

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:03.520 align:middle line:84%
I take the T in the sun, and
I take the T in the rain.

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:06.240 align:middle line:84%
It's important to understand
the outcome has to capture

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:07.420 align:middle line:90%
everything about my choice.

00:42:07.420 --> 00:42:08.940 align:middle line:90%
It's not directly my choice.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:10.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm not directly
choosing outcomes.

00:42:10.700 --> 00:42:13.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm choosing effectively
lotteries over outcomes.

00:42:13.640 --> 00:42:15.640 align:middle line:84%
And the outcome has to
capture everything that's

00:42:15.640 --> 00:42:17.880 align:middle line:90%
relevant in the situation.

00:42:17.880 --> 00:42:20.920 align:middle line:84%
So we have this set of
outcomes or consequences.

00:42:20.920 --> 00:42:23.960 align:middle line:84%
And then we have the Von
Neumann-Morgenstern utility

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:26.680 align:middle line:90%
function little u.

00:42:26.680 --> 00:42:28.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, just as we saw
over here, little u

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:31.400 align:middle line:84%
assigned a number to each
of our four outcomes.

00:42:31.400 --> 00:42:36.680 align:middle line:84%
So U is going to be a
function from Z to R.

00:42:36.680 --> 00:42:38.680 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to use this
function notation a lot

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:39.640 align:middle line:90%
in the course.

00:42:39.640 --> 00:42:41.760 align:middle line:84%
When I put a letter
and then a colon,

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:43.520 align:middle line:84%
that means this
is a function that

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:46.340 align:middle line:84%
assigns to every
item in this set,

00:42:46.340 --> 00:42:49.240 align:middle line:84%
every object in this set,
something in this set.

00:42:49.240 --> 00:42:52.932 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, it assigns
to every outcome Z is--

00:42:52.932 --> 00:42:54.640 align:middle line:84%
I'll write it like
this underneath-- what

00:42:54.640 --> 00:42:59.160 align:middle line:84%
we'll call u of Z. And u
of Z is the cardinal Von

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:04.920 align:middle line:84%
Neumann-Morgenstern utility
I get from the outcome Z.

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:09.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, given the set of outcomes
and my Von Neumann-Morgenstern

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:14.320 align:middle line:84%
utility, we can consider
my expected utility.

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:15.500 align:middle line:90%
Let me write it this.

00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:20.480 align:middle line:90%


00:43:20.480 --> 00:43:22.710 align:middle line:84%
So my expected
utility is the big U.

00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:24.460 align:middle line:84%
And what is this going
to be defined over?

00:43:24.460 --> 00:43:27.043 align:middle line:84%
It's really crucial that this
is defined over a different set.

00:43:27.043 --> 00:43:29.220 align:middle line:84%
So what do I assign
expected utilities to?

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:32.220 align:middle line:90%


00:43:32.220 --> 00:43:32.720 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:34.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Would it
be a set of actions?

00:43:34.960 --> 00:43:37.720 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So in this context,
it was a set of actions.

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:39.520 align:middle line:84%
But in this abstract
model-- good question.

00:43:39.520 --> 00:43:40.102 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:40.102 --> 00:43:41.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like the
state you're in.

00:43:41.560 --> 00:43:44.780 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So I would say-- may
be a bit of a vague question.

00:43:44.780 --> 00:43:48.330 align:middle line:84%
I would say the set of
lotteries over outcomes.

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:49.080 align:middle line:90%
So let's be clear.

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:51.140 align:middle line:84%
Here, you're right, we
were choosing actions.

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:53.360 align:middle line:84%
We chose whether to
walk or take the MBTA.

00:43:53.360 --> 00:43:56.200 align:middle line:84%
But the way we think about those
actions in this abstract setting

00:43:56.200 --> 00:43:58.300 align:middle line:84%
is that each one
corresponds to a lottery.

00:43:58.300 --> 00:44:01.160 align:middle line:84%
Walking is the lottery
where with probability p,

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:04.440 align:middle line:84%
I walk in the sun, and
with probability 1 minus p,

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:06.120 align:middle line:90%
I walk in the rain.

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:08.880 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, the MBTA
is a different lottery

00:44:08.880 --> 00:44:11.100 align:middle line:84%
where with probability
p, I get this outcome,

00:44:11.100 --> 00:44:13.540 align:middle line:84%
and with probability 1
minus p, I get this outcome.

00:44:13.540 --> 00:44:15.480 align:middle line:84%
So we need some
notation for lotteries.

00:44:15.480 --> 00:44:19.830 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use throughout
the course delta of Z.

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:21.580 align:middle line:84%
I don't want the
notation to scare people.

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:22.520 align:middle line:84%
It's just going
to make it easier

00:44:22.520 --> 00:44:23.980 align:middle line:90%
for us to talk about things.

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:25.740 align:middle line:90%
So Z is our set of outcomes.

00:44:25.740 --> 00:44:29.880 align:middle line:84%
Delta of Z is the set of
lotteries over these outcomes.

00:44:29.880 --> 00:44:34.080 align:middle line:84%
So in this context where we only
had two outcomes, a lottery just

00:44:34.080 --> 00:44:37.260 align:middle line:84%
specified two numbers,
p and 1 minus p.

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:39.497 align:middle line:84%
And because those
numbers sum to 1,

00:44:39.497 --> 00:44:41.330 align:middle line:84%
it was really only one
number that mattered.

00:44:41.330 --> 00:44:44.350 align:middle line:84%
Once we knew p, we were
able to calculate 1 minus p.

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:46.790 align:middle line:84%
But in general, we're
looking at a situation

00:44:46.790 --> 00:44:49.350 align:middle line:84%
where there are m outcomes
instead of just two.

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:50.530 align:middle line:90%
So what is a lottery?

00:44:50.530 --> 00:44:53.110 align:middle line:90%


00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:57.950 align:middle line:84%
So it contains what
I'll call lotteries.

00:44:57.950 --> 00:45:00.870 align:middle line:84%
And again, I should be clear
the use of lottery colloquially

00:45:00.870 --> 00:45:01.955 align:middle line:90%
is different from in econ.

00:45:01.955 --> 00:45:04.330 align:middle line:84%
We're not literally talking
about buying lottery tickets.

00:45:04.330 --> 00:45:05.790 align:middle line:84%
We're using this
more abstractly.

00:45:05.790 --> 00:45:10.910 align:middle line:84%
It's a vector p that specifies
the probability of each

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:13.030 align:middle line:90%
of these outcomes.

00:45:13.030 --> 00:45:16.350 align:middle line:90%
So p1 to pm.

00:45:16.350 --> 00:45:18.910 align:middle line:84%
The lottery says
with probability p1,

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:20.990 align:middle line:90%
you get consequence Z1.

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:24.510 align:middle line:84%
With probability pm,
you get consequence Zm.

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:25.570 align:middle line:90%
These are probabilities.

00:45:25.570 --> 00:45:27.170 align:middle line:84%
So what has to be
true about them?

00:45:27.170 --> 00:45:28.450 align:middle line:90%
They have to be non-negative.

00:45:28.450 --> 00:45:30.070 align:middle line:84%
You can't have
negative probabilities.

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:33.310 align:middle line:90%
And they have to sum to 1.

00:45:33.310 --> 00:45:41.470 align:middle line:84%
So say p1 up to pm are
greater than or equal to 0.

00:45:41.470 --> 00:45:45.770 align:middle line:90%
And p1 plus pm equals 1.

00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:50.190 align:middle line:90%


00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:53.750 align:middle line:84%
So delta of Z is the set of all
of these probability vectors

00:45:53.750 --> 00:45:58.510 align:middle line:84%
or lotteries that
satisfy these properties.

00:45:58.510 --> 00:46:00.310 align:middle line:84%
And now our expected
utility function

00:46:00.310 --> 00:46:07.830 align:middle line:84%
u is a function
from delta Z to R.

00:46:07.830 --> 00:46:10.630 align:middle line:84%
It says, for any lottery,
what is my expected

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:12.630 align:middle line:90%
utility from that lottery?

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:14.870 align:middle line:84%
So again, using
the notation above,

00:46:14.870 --> 00:46:19.710 align:middle line:84%
we would call a lottery p,
and that would go to u of p.

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:21.370 align:middle line:84%
So this distinction
is crucial, right?

00:46:21.370 --> 00:46:23.810 align:middle line:84%
A VNM utility says
for each outcome,

00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:26.070 align:middle line:84%
what is my utility
from that outcome?

00:46:26.070 --> 00:46:30.550 align:middle line:84%
And expected utility big U says
for each lottery over outcomes,

00:46:30.550 --> 00:46:33.750 align:middle line:84%
what is my expected
utility from that lottery?

00:46:33.750 --> 00:46:34.693 align:middle line:90%
Yes, question?

00:46:34.693 --> 00:46:36.110 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just to
clarify, when we

00:46:36.110 --> 00:46:39.390 align:middle line:84%
say lotteries, that's what
we're modeling your decision as

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:41.530 align:middle line:84%
is opting into a
specific lottery.

00:46:41.530 --> 00:46:44.350 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So in the
face of uncertainty,

00:46:44.350 --> 00:46:47.270 align:middle line:84%
we're going to imagine that
each choice that you make

00:46:47.270 --> 00:46:49.717 align:middle line:84%
is going to induce some
lottery of outcomes.

00:46:49.717 --> 00:46:52.050 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to represent
the choice by that lottery.

00:46:52.050 --> 00:46:54.738 align:middle line:84%
This is an important point,
because it could be that--

00:46:54.738 --> 00:46:56.530 align:middle line:84%
yeah, there's an embedded
assumption there.

00:46:56.530 --> 00:46:57.870 align:middle line:84%
But I'll just say yeah,
that's what we're assuming.

00:46:57.870 --> 00:46:58.550 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:46:58.550 --> 00:47:01.127 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you just say
what u would be in this case?

00:47:01.127 --> 00:47:01.710 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yes.

00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:03.270 align:middle line:84%
So let me write the
definition and then I'll

00:47:03.270 --> 00:47:03.930 align:middle line:90%
give you an example.

00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:04.430 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:47:04.430 --> 00:47:05.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The little one.

00:47:05.550 --> 00:47:06.175 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:47:06.175 --> 00:47:07.270 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, exactly.

00:47:07.270 --> 00:47:09.130 align:middle line:84%
So let's first give
the general definition.

00:47:09.130 --> 00:47:10.338 align:middle line:90%
Then I'll go to this example.

00:47:10.338 --> 00:47:12.910 align:middle line:90%
So u of p equals what?

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:16.590 align:middle line:84%
Well, I'm just going to sum
over all the possible outcomes.

00:47:16.590 --> 00:47:18.128 align:middle line:90%
There are m outcomes.

00:47:18.128 --> 00:47:19.670 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
take-- and let me not

00:47:19.670 --> 00:47:21.190 align:middle line:84%
use summation notation,
because I think that makes

00:47:21.190 --> 00:47:22.482 align:middle line:90%
it just seem harder than it is.

00:47:22.482 --> 00:47:25.850 align:middle line:90%
So let me write p1 u of Z1.

00:47:25.850 --> 00:47:34.230 align:middle line:90%


00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:38.550 align:middle line:84%
So I'm saying with probability
p1, I get outcome Z1.

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:39.970 align:middle line:90%
And this is my utility.

00:47:39.970 --> 00:47:42.230 align:middle line:84%
And I sum it all the way
up until I get to pn.

00:47:42.230 --> 00:47:44.190 align:middle line:84%
This is the probability
of outcome n.

00:47:44.190 --> 00:47:46.030 align:middle line:90%
And I get utility this.

00:47:46.030 --> 00:47:48.270 align:middle line:84%
So if we wanted to
really formally write

00:47:48.270 --> 00:47:50.790 align:middle line:90%
this in the example over here--

00:47:50.790 --> 00:47:58.230 align:middle line:84%
so in the example,
walking corresponded

00:47:58.230 --> 00:47:59.522 align:middle line:90%
to a particular lottery, right?

00:47:59.522 --> 00:48:01.480 align:middle line:84%
Now, if I really want to
write out the example,

00:48:01.480 --> 00:48:03.450 align:middle line:84%
I need to write out all
four of these things.

00:48:03.450 --> 00:48:11.150 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll call
these Z1, Z2, Z3, Z4.

00:48:11.150 --> 00:48:11.650 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:48:11.650 --> 00:48:14.750 align:middle line:84%
These are the four
consequences of my actions.

00:48:14.750 --> 00:48:17.570 align:middle line:90%
Now, what is walking here?

00:48:17.570 --> 00:48:19.470 align:middle line:84%
We need to specify
four numbers--

00:48:19.470 --> 00:48:23.550 align:middle line:90%
p1, p2, p3, p4.

00:48:23.550 --> 00:48:26.630 align:middle line:84%
What are these four
numbers in this example?

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:31.510 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I walk, I either get
this box with probability p

00:48:31.510 --> 00:48:34.390 align:middle line:84%
or this box with
probability 1 minus p.

00:48:34.390 --> 00:48:37.870 align:middle line:84%
So of these four numbers,
two of them are 0.

00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:40.930 align:middle line:90%
p3 and p4 are both 0.

00:48:40.930 --> 00:48:44.050 align:middle line:90%


00:48:44.050 --> 00:48:44.550 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:48:44.550 --> 00:48:48.107 align:middle line:84%
Because it's impossible
for me to get

00:48:48.107 --> 00:48:49.190 align:middle line:90%
one of those consequences.

00:48:49.190 --> 00:48:50.565 align:middle line:84%
If I choose to
walk home, there's

00:48:50.565 --> 00:48:52.210 align:middle line:90%
no way-- oh, I got unlucky.

00:48:52.210 --> 00:48:55.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm now on the T. We're not
imagining that can happen.

00:48:55.510 --> 00:48:58.030 align:middle line:84%
What can happen is you can
either walk home when it gets

00:48:58.030 --> 00:48:59.870 align:middle line:84%
wet or you walk
home and you don't.

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:02.530 align:middle line:90%
So we have Z1 is going to be p.

00:49:02.530 --> 00:49:05.910 align:middle line:90%


00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:07.830 align:middle line:90%
1 minus p here.

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:13.030 align:middle line:84%
And if we compute it, what
is u of this particular p

00:49:13.030 --> 00:49:14.070 align:middle line:90%
going to be?

00:49:14.070 --> 00:49:21.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, we go through and we
get pu of Z1 plus 1 minus pu

00:49:21.830 --> 00:49:23.968 align:middle line:90%
of Z2 plus 0 plus 0.

00:49:23.968 --> 00:49:25.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to
include the last two

00:49:25.510 --> 00:49:27.350 align:middle line:90%
terms because they disappear.

00:49:27.350 --> 00:49:34.780 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be exactly
what we said before, p times 7

00:49:34.780 --> 00:49:38.357 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 minus p times 2.

00:49:38.357 --> 00:49:39.440 align:middle line:90%
Which we already computed.

00:49:39.440 --> 00:49:40.440 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that?

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:43.820 align:middle line:90%


00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:44.780 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:49:44.780 --> 00:49:47.072 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So when we're
actually getting the value that

00:49:47.072 --> 00:49:49.740 align:middle line:84%
goes from p to a
real number, there's

00:49:49.740 --> 00:49:53.020 align:middle line:84%
already an implicit choice, like
here implicitly we're walking

00:49:53.020 --> 00:49:55.195 align:middle line:84%
and we're not calculating
this particular T.

00:49:55.195 --> 00:49:55.820 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:49:55.820 --> 00:49:59.813 align:middle line:84%
So what I would say is in
principle, you can evaluate--

00:49:59.813 --> 00:50:02.480 align:middle line:84%
and this is the key distinction
between preferences and choices.

00:50:02.480 --> 00:50:04.060 align:middle line:84%
In principle, you
could say, if I

00:50:04.060 --> 00:50:06.980 align:middle line:84%
were offered a lottery between
these outcomes, this lottery

00:50:06.980 --> 00:50:09.180 align:middle line:84%
versus this lottery,
what would I prefer?

00:50:09.180 --> 00:50:10.440 align:middle line:90%
Those are your preferences.

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:13.100 align:middle line:84%
But in reality, you're given
a limited menu of lotteries

00:50:13.100 --> 00:50:14.520 align:middle line:84%
that you can
actually choose from.

00:50:14.520 --> 00:50:16.200 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to make a
choice between those lotteries.

00:50:16.200 --> 00:50:19.220 align:middle line:84%
So I could say in my head, what
would I prefer between a stock

00:50:19.220 --> 00:50:22.460 align:middle line:84%
that always pays a return of
1,000% and a stock that always

00:50:22.460 --> 00:50:23.835 align:middle line:84%
pays a return of
negative 1,000%?

00:50:23.835 --> 00:50:25.002 align:middle line:90%
I know which one I'd prefer.

00:50:25.002 --> 00:50:26.380 align:middle line:84%
And I can compute
my preferences.

00:50:26.380 --> 00:50:27.900 align:middle line:84%
But there may not
actually be a company

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:29.180 align:middle line:84%
listed on the stock
market that is

00:50:29.180 --> 00:50:30.320 align:middle line:90%
going to give me that lottery.

00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:32.237 align:middle line:84%
In reality, I'm going
to face a limited choice

00:50:32.237 --> 00:50:34.000 align:middle line:90%
set, a limited menu of options.

00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:35.920 align:middle line:84%
Each of those options
will induce a lottery,

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:37.740 align:middle line:84%
and I'll make a choice
among this smaller

00:50:37.740 --> 00:50:38.920 align:middle line:90%
collection of lotteries.

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:41.740 align:middle line:84%
In this case, that collection
had two lotteries, the walking

00:50:41.740 --> 00:50:43.480 align:middle line:84%
lottery and the
taking the T lottery.

00:50:43.480 --> 00:50:45.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When you're
calculating this function,

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:48.646 align:middle line:90%
you've already chosen a lottery.

00:50:48.646 --> 00:50:50.740 align:middle line:84%
Or you calculate this
function based off of that.

00:50:50.740 --> 00:50:51.920 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: I would
say it's a function,

00:50:51.920 --> 00:50:53.960 align:middle line:84%
so it's defined for
every single lottery.

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:56.860 align:middle line:84%
I can say for every single
lottery what my expected utility

00:50:56.860 --> 00:50:59.100 align:middle line:84%
is, even for lotteries that
I may not have the option

00:50:59.100 --> 00:51:01.740 align:middle line:90%
to choose in a given situation.

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:02.970 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:51:02.970 --> 00:51:05.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So even though p
is determined on our beliefs,

00:51:05.540 --> 00:51:07.660 align:middle line:84%
when it's a function
of big U, do we just

00:51:07.660 --> 00:51:11.580 align:middle line:84%
take it as exogenous and
optimize based off of the things

00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:12.520 align:middle line:90%
that we can control?

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:13.520 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Good question.

00:51:13.520 --> 00:51:14.820 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to
be a big difference

00:51:14.820 --> 00:51:17.480 align:middle line:84%
between individual decision
making and interactive decision

00:51:17.480 --> 00:51:17.980 align:middle line:90%
making.

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:20.420 align:middle line:84%
In individual decision making,
all of this uncertainty

00:51:20.420 --> 00:51:21.720 align:middle line:90%
is just going to be exogenous.

00:51:21.720 --> 00:51:23.200 align:middle line:84%
And that's the model
I've written down.

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:25.242 align:middle line:84%
But we'll see when we get
to interactive decision

00:51:25.242 --> 00:51:27.580 align:middle line:84%
making, well, how do I form
beliefs about the choices

00:51:27.580 --> 00:51:28.600 align:middle line:90%
that other people make?

00:51:28.600 --> 00:51:29.900 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going
to have to impose more

00:51:29.900 --> 00:51:31.025 align:middle line:90%
structure on these beliefs.

00:51:31.025 --> 00:51:33.608 align:middle line:84%
But for the individual context,
we think of them as exogenous.

00:51:33.608 --> 00:51:35.442 align:middle line:84%
Through introspection,
you form your beliefs

00:51:35.442 --> 00:51:37.560 align:middle line:84%
about what nature does,
not what other players do.

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:38.060 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:51:38.060 --> 00:51:39.260 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:51:39.260 --> 00:51:42.660 align:middle line:84%
OK, so one final thing
in the last five minutes.

00:51:42.660 --> 00:51:46.420 align:middle line:84%
There's a special case, which
is of a lot of importance.

00:51:46.420 --> 00:51:54.140 align:middle line:84%
And I'll say the
key special case

00:51:54.140 --> 00:51:56.160 align:middle line:84%
is going to be what we
call money lotteries.

00:51:56.160 --> 00:52:00.560 align:middle line:90%


00:52:00.560 --> 00:52:02.060 align:middle line:84%
So these are more
like the lotteries

00:52:02.060 --> 00:52:04.580 align:middle line:90%
that you hear about in reality.

00:52:04.580 --> 00:52:06.560 align:middle line:84%
And why do I call
it a special case?

00:52:06.560 --> 00:52:09.580 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's the special case
where Z, the set of outcomes,

00:52:09.580 --> 00:52:12.580 align:middle line:84%
is just a set of
monetary rewards.

00:52:12.580 --> 00:52:17.700 align:middle line:84%
So I'll say Z is equal to R. And
this means the real number line.

00:52:17.700 --> 00:52:19.162 align:middle line:90%
Now, before I said Z was finite.

00:52:19.162 --> 00:52:20.620 align:middle line:84%
Here I'm going to
be a little loose

00:52:20.620 --> 00:52:22.340 align:middle line:90%
and allow Z to be infinite.

00:52:22.340 --> 00:52:25.680 align:middle line:84%
So the outcome here is
I get $1, I get $10,

00:52:25.680 --> 00:52:26.680 align:middle line:90%
I get a billion dollars.

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:27.762 align:middle line:90%
These are all outcomes.

00:52:27.762 --> 00:52:28.720 align:middle line:90%
So what is the lottery?

00:52:28.720 --> 00:52:31.660 align:middle line:90%
Let's consider two lotteries.

00:52:31.660 --> 00:52:35.520 align:middle line:90%
One lottery maybe pays $10.

00:52:35.520 --> 00:52:55.780 align:middle line:90%


00:52:55.780 --> 00:52:57.700 align:middle line:84%
So let's now consider
two lotteries.

00:52:57.700 --> 00:52:59.672 align:middle line:84%
Now here, with
infinitely many things,

00:52:59.672 --> 00:53:00.880 align:middle line:90%
it could be more complicated.

00:53:00.880 --> 00:53:02.140 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to
look at lotteries

00:53:02.140 --> 00:53:04.340 align:middle line:84%
where there's a small number
of things that could actually

00:53:04.340 --> 00:53:05.000 align:middle line:90%
happen.

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:06.508 align:middle line:84%
So under this
lottery, there's only

00:53:06.508 --> 00:53:07.800 align:middle line:90%
two outcomes that could happen.

00:53:07.800 --> 00:53:10.060 align:middle line:84%
Every other outcome
has probability 0.

00:53:10.060 --> 00:53:15.460 align:middle line:84%
Under this lottery, with
probability 0.99, I get $10.

00:53:15.460 --> 00:53:18.040 align:middle line:84%
And with probability
0.01, I get nothing.

00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:19.340 align:middle line:90%
I get 0.

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:24.380 align:middle line:84%
With this lottery, I get $1,000
with probability 0.01 and I get

00:53:24.380 --> 00:53:26.620 align:middle line:90%
0 with probability 0.99.

00:53:26.620 --> 00:53:27.840 align:middle line:90%
So two different lotteries.

00:53:27.840 --> 00:53:29.240 align:middle line:90%
They fit within our framework.

00:53:29.240 --> 00:53:31.920 align:middle line:84%
And we can compute our expected
utility of these lotteries.

00:53:31.920 --> 00:53:32.420 align:middle line:90%
How?

00:53:32.420 --> 00:53:37.620 align:middle line:84%
Well, to do that, we need our
VNM utility function, which

00:53:37.620 --> 00:53:41.140 align:middle line:84%
is a function, remember from
Z to R. But in this case,

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:47.940 align:middle line:84%
Z is just R. But let's
understand, this is money

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:49.400 align:middle line:90%
and this is utility.

00:53:49.400 --> 00:53:53.100 align:middle line:90%


00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:56.040 align:middle line:84%
So which of these
lotteries would you prefer?

00:53:56.040 --> 00:53:57.040 align:middle line:90%
There's no right answer.

00:53:57.040 --> 00:53:59.660 align:middle line:84%
Again, preferences
are not irrational.

00:53:59.660 --> 00:54:02.600 align:middle line:84%
But what's your
preference between these?

00:54:02.600 --> 00:54:06.840 align:middle line:90%


00:54:06.840 --> 00:54:07.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:54:07.340 --> 00:54:08.380 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I like the first one.

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:09.060 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: You like
the first one?

00:54:09.060 --> 00:54:09.220 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:54:09.220 --> 00:54:10.400 align:middle line:90%
I think most people would.

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:12.980 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

00:54:12.980 --> 00:54:19.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because I don't know,
it seems like 1,000 divided

00:54:19.660 --> 00:54:22.360 align:middle line:84%
by whatever seems
to be about 10,

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:25.100 align:middle line:84%
and then it feels more
certain because it's 0.99.

00:54:25.100 --> 00:54:25.900 align:middle line:90%
But I don't know.

00:54:25.900 --> 00:54:26.200 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:54:26.200 --> 00:54:27.280 align:middle line:84%
So that's a great way
of thinking about it.

00:54:27.280 --> 00:54:28.738 align:middle line:84%
So the first thing
you might say is

00:54:28.738 --> 00:54:31.100 align:middle line:84%
let's compare the expected
values of these lotteries.

00:54:31.100 --> 00:54:34.020 align:middle line:84%
The expected value of
this is exactly $10.

00:54:34.020 --> 00:54:37.860 align:middle line:84%
100th of 1,000 has
expected value $10.

00:54:37.860 --> 00:54:39.720 align:middle line:84%
What's the expected
value of this?

00:54:39.720 --> 00:54:42.060 align:middle line:90%
It's $9.90.

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:45.260 align:middle line:84%
So this has a lower
monetary expected value.

00:54:45.260 --> 00:54:48.060 align:middle line:84%
But despite it having a lower
monetary expected value,

00:54:48.060 --> 00:54:50.940 align:middle line:84%
it might have a higher
expected utility.

00:54:50.940 --> 00:54:54.680 align:middle line:84%
And many people would choose
this lottery over this lottery

00:54:54.680 --> 00:54:57.580 align:middle line:84%
exactly because of what
you said, it's safer.

00:54:57.580 --> 00:55:01.060 align:middle line:84%
So what's key is through our
Von Neumann-Morgenstern utility

00:55:01.060 --> 00:55:04.217 align:middle line:84%
function u, when we
evaluate lotteries,

00:55:04.217 --> 00:55:06.300 align:middle line:84%
we don't just compare the
expected monetary amount

00:55:06.300 --> 00:55:08.740 align:middle line:84%
of the lotteries, we
compare the expected utility

00:55:08.740 --> 00:55:11.700 align:middle line:90%
between the lotteries.

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:14.420 align:middle line:84%
And what you're
describing is what

00:55:14.420 --> 00:55:18.810 align:middle line:84%
a lot of people feature in this
context is, I'll say often,

00:55:18.810 --> 00:55:23.090 align:middle line:90%
u is a concave function.

00:55:23.090 --> 00:55:27.330 align:middle line:84%
So if I were to draw
u for a lot of people,

00:55:27.330 --> 00:55:29.930 align:middle line:90%
their u is something like this.

00:55:29.930 --> 00:55:32.090 align:middle line:84%
And when u is concave,
it means people

00:55:32.090 --> 00:55:34.790 align:middle line:90%
are what we call risk averse.

00:55:34.790 --> 00:55:37.970 align:middle line:90%


00:55:37.970 --> 00:55:40.610 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, you
exhibited risk aversion.

00:55:40.610 --> 00:55:44.210 align:middle line:84%
You preferred this
lottery to this lottery,

00:55:44.210 --> 00:55:48.090 align:middle line:84%
even though it had a
smaller expected value.

00:55:48.090 --> 00:55:51.410 align:middle line:84%
And if we want to
formally define

00:55:51.410 --> 00:55:55.690 align:middle line:84%
what does risk aversion
mean, a risk averse person

00:55:55.690 --> 00:55:59.970 align:middle line:84%
is someone who always
prefers a certainty

00:55:59.970 --> 00:56:03.155 align:middle line:84%
at the expected value of a
lottery to the lottery itself.

00:56:03.155 --> 00:56:05.530 align:middle line:84%
So let me just write this
mathematically and then explain

00:56:05.530 --> 00:56:06.030 align:middle line:90%
it.

00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:12.090 align:middle line:90%


00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:16.350 align:middle line:84%
Someone is risk averse if
deciding between a lottery p--

00:56:16.350 --> 00:56:17.910 align:middle line:90%
let's take this as an example.

00:56:17.910 --> 00:56:19.410 align:middle line:90%
This is one lottery p.

00:56:19.410 --> 00:56:22.450 align:middle line:84%
And another lottery that
pays the expected value of p

00:56:22.450 --> 00:56:23.690 align:middle line:90%
with certainty.

00:56:23.690 --> 00:56:26.747 align:middle line:84%
In this case, that
would be exactly $10.

00:56:26.747 --> 00:56:28.330 align:middle line:84%
Someone who's risk
averse would always

00:56:28.330 --> 00:56:31.290 align:middle line:84%
prefer getting $10
for sure to getting

00:56:31.290 --> 00:56:34.670 align:middle line:84%
a lottery whose expected
monetary amount was $10.

00:56:34.670 --> 00:56:36.610 align:middle line:84%
And mathematically,
that's captured

00:56:36.610 --> 00:56:40.052 align:middle line:84%
by the concavity of the
utility function here, u.

00:56:40.052 --> 00:56:41.510 align:middle line:84%
For this to be a
strict preference,

00:56:41.510 --> 00:56:43.090 align:middle line:90%
we need p to be non-degenerate.

00:56:43.090 --> 00:56:45.630 align:middle line:84%
1p would be you get
something with probability 1.

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:47.550 align:middle line:84%
So as long as it's
non-degenerate,

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:49.870 align:middle line:84%
this is the meaning
of risk aversion.

00:56:49.870 --> 00:56:51.830 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of answers
a puzzle, I mean,

00:56:51.830 --> 00:56:53.790 align:middle line:84%
going back to the 1700s
where people said,

00:56:53.790 --> 00:56:55.808 align:middle line:84%
why would you choose this
lottery over this one?

00:56:55.808 --> 00:56:57.350 align:middle line:84%
This one has a higher
expected value.

00:56:57.350 --> 00:56:59.570 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is, well,
your utility function

00:56:59.570 --> 00:57:01.390 align:middle line:90%
is not the same as money.

00:57:01.390 --> 00:57:03.310 align:middle line:84%
You don't maximize
expected money,

00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:04.910 align:middle line:90%
you maximize expected utility.

00:57:04.910 --> 00:57:08.370 align:middle line:84%
And some people's utilities
can be concave like this.

00:57:08.370 --> 00:57:12.120 align:middle line:84%
So let me stop there and I
will see everyone next week.

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:16.000 align:middle line:90%